window.SAME_FOREST_SPREADS = [{"caption":"the cloud he quit fighting comes apart, and the loose ends turn out to be reins.","date":"2026-09-22","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g22' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g22)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><circle cx='170' cy='170' r='15.0'/><path d='M170 185.0 L 170 219.0'/><path d='M170 195.0 L 154.0 213.0 M170 195.0 L 186.0 213.0'/><path d='M170 219.0 L 140.0 229.0 L 166.0 237.0 M170 219.0 L 200.0 229.0 L 174.0 237.0'/><path d='M110 90 q 20 -40 60 -20 q 30 -30 60 0 q 40 -10 30 30 q 20 30 -20 30 q -20 30 -60 10 q -40 20 -60 -10 q -30 -10 -10 -40 z' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.85'/><path d='M126 78 q 30 20 60 -6 q 30 24 56 0 q 20 22 -8 34 q -34 14 -60 -4 q -30 18 -48 -10' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M262 100 q 40 10 80 40 M258 118 q 50 20 90 60 M250 134 q 40 30 70 80' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><path d='M342 140 L 590 168 M348 178 L 596 172 M320 214 L 600 178' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><rect x='620' y='190' width='90' height='50' rx='4'/><circle cx='640' cy='258' r='16'/><circle cx='695' cy='258' r='16'/><path d='M620 212 L 556 214'/><ellipse cx='520' cy='218' rx='42' ry='18'/><path d='M556 208 L 578 176'/><ellipse cx='586' cy='172' rx='15' ry='8'/><path d='M498 234 L 492 266 M512 236 L 510 266 M532 236 L 536 266 M548 232 L 556 266 M480 214 q -18 6 -20 22' stroke-width='2.5'/><circle cx='665' cy='110' r='15.0'/><path d='M665 125.0 L 665 159.0'/><path d='M665 135.0 L 651.0 119.0 M665 135.0 L 679.0 119.0'/><path d='M665 159.0 L 653.0 199.0 M665 159.0 L 677.0 199.0'/><path d='M690 120 q 30 30 0 60' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M690 120 L 690 180' stroke-width='1.5' opacity='.7'/><\/g><g transform='translate(580,185.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"mingyur rinpoche, the joy of living · read chapter 1, he tells it himself","sourceRef":"rinpoche","text":"sit down.\n[[rinpoche|mingyur rinpoche]] had [[panic|panic attacks]] as a kid. real ones, the kind where you think you're dying. at thirteen he went into a [[retreat|three year retreat]] and for the first year they got worse.\nthen he quit fighting them. he let the panic in and watched it. it fell apart in a few days. it had needed the fight to stay alive."},"mode":"disagree","question":"is this the kind of sitting that dissolves the thing, or the kind that keeps it company?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side, along with the great-uncle who raised him and the teacher who taught him to shoot.\nhe asks krishna to drive out between the armies so he can look. he looks, his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down in the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice except krishna, for about three verses, and then even he moves on to something better. the sitting down is real. so is the getting up. the book needs both.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1 for sitting down, chapter 2 for the rest."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic. eighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts.\nhindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts talking, and the first thing he says is not gentle. read the first ten verses before the famous ones and you'll see the tone the rest of the book is correcting for.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2, verses 1 through 10, then keep going. any translation you can hold."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't.\nbut before any of that, right at the top of chapter two, he's just a friend watching another friend fall apart at the worst possible moment, and he says the thing friends say. get up. this isn't you.\nthe kindness comes after. the first thing out of his mouth is a shove.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita 2:1-10 for the shove. the rest of chapter 2 for what comes after it."},"panic":{"lines":"he describes them starting around age seven or eight. his heart would race, he couldn't breathe, he was sure he was about to die. they came most in crowds and when he had to sit in the front at ceremonies, which as a recognized young teacher was constantly.\nhis father taught him to meditate at nine, partly for this. it helped and it didn't. the panic was still there, waiting.\nwhat finally worked is the part he tells over and over, because it's the whole teaching: he stopped treating the panic as an enemy and started treating it as a guest. he looked straight at it. and it turned out to be made of nothing in particular, once it wasn't being pushed against.","name":"the panic attacks","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1, and the introduction. he tells it plainly."},"retreat":{"lines":"in tibetan buddhism there's a traditional intensive: three years, three months, three days, in a closed retreat with a small group, doing nothing but practice. it's how the tradition trains its teachers.\nhe went in at thirteen, the youngest in his group, at sherab ling monastery in india. he says the first year was the worst of his life. the panic had nowhere to go and neither did he.\nsomewhere in that year he made the choice to stop fighting, and by his account the attacks were gone within days and never came back.\nhe was so young when he came out that they sent him back in as the retreat master for the next group. he was seventeen.","name":"the three year retreat","where":"the joy of living, chapter 1. he keeps the retreat details short; the turn is the point."},"rinpoche":{"lines":"a tibetan buddhist teacher, born in nepal in 1975, the son of one of the great meditation masters of the last century, tulku urgyen rinpoche. rinpoche is a title, it means precious one. he was recognized as a reincarnated teacher as a small boy.\nnone of that stopped the panic attacks. he's open about it, which is rare for someone in his position, and it's why people trust him.\nhe's been wired up in neuroscience labs more than almost any meditator alive, which is where the newspapers got the line about the happiest man in the world. he laughs at that.\nin 2011 he walked out of his monastery with no money and no plan and wandered india as a beggar for four and a half years. he nearly died of food poisoning in the first few weeks. he came back and wrote about it. he runs a network of meditation centers called tergar.","name":"yongey mingyur rinpoche","where":"the joy of living, 2007, for the panic and the retreat. in love with the world, 2019, for the wandering years."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:2-3 · read the first ten verses of chapter 2, before he gets kind","sourceRef":"gita","text":"get up.\nthat's what [[krishna]] says to [[arjuna]] the second he opens his mouth. before the philosophy, before any of it. don't be a coward, it doesn't suit you, get up.\nsitting with it is not the same as sitting it out. sometimes the thing you're watching so carefully is just you, not moving."},"theme":"sit down, get up","id":"2026-09-22"},{"caption":"the pieces of the pot are the stones of the path.","date":"2026-09-23","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g23' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g23)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><circle cx='140' cy='120' r='15.0'/><path d='M140 135.0 L 140 169.0'/><path d='M140 145.0 L 124.0 163.0 M140 145.0 L 156.0 163.0'/><path d='M140 169.0 L 128.0 209.0 M140 169.0 L 152.0 209.0'/><path d='M110 175 q 30 -20 60 0 l -6 30 q -24 8 -48 0 z'/><path d='M200 268 l 14 -10 l 10 12 z M240 274 l 12 -8 l 8 10 z M290 270 l 14 -9 l 9 11 z M340 272 l 12 -8 l 9 10 z' stroke-width='2.5'/><path d='M0 288 L 400 288' opacity='.4'/><path d='M405 274 q 14 -10 28 0 M445 276 q 14 -10 28 0 M485 274 q 14 -10 28 0 M525 276 q 14 -10 28 0' stroke-width='2.5'/><circle cx='660' cy='120' r='15'/><path d='M660 135 L 660 190'/><path d='M660 150 L 646 168 M660 150 L 674 168'/><path d='M660 190 L 704 190 L 704 226 M700 226 L 714 226'/><path d='M640 150 L 640 192 L 700 192'/><circle cx='652' cy='232' r='30'/><circle cx='712' cy='250' r='10'/><path d='M400 288 L 800 288' opacity='.4'/><\/g><g transform='translate(668,172.0) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"richard rohr, falling upward · read the introduction, it's the whole argument","sourceRef":"falling","text":"you don't grow from doing it right.\n[[rohr]]'s whole book [[falling|falling upward]] is one point. the first half of your life builds the container. the second half only starts when something breaks it.\nnobody signs up for the breaking. you get thrown."},"mode":"agree","question":"what broke that you're still trying to glue back?","refs":{"falling":{"lines":"rohr's argument is that there are two halves to a life, and they are not the same job.\nthe first half is building the container. identity, career, family, beliefs, a sense of being somebody. you're supposed to do this, and you're supposed to take it seriously.\nthe second half is what the container was for. and here's the catch he's built the book around: almost nobody moves into the second half on purpose. you get moved. failure, loss, illness, a marriage ending, something you can't fix. he calls it necessary suffering.\nthe title is the point. the way up is the way down. you fall into the second half of your life. you don't climb.","name":"falling upward","where":"falling upward: a spirituality for the two halves of life. chapter 5, on necessary suffering, if you only read one."},"ramdass":{"lines":"born richard alpert, a harvard psychology professor who got fired in 1963 alongside timothy leary for giving psychedelics to students.\nhe went to india in 1967 looking for somebody who knew what the drugs had shown him, and found an old man in a blanket named neem karoli baba, who read his mind and told him things nobody could have known. he never really came back. the old man named him ram dass, servant of god.\nhe wrote be here now in 1971, a book that looks like a hippie art project and is still in print because it works. he spent the next fifty years talking about love, service, and dying, and he was funny the whole time.\nhe died in maui in december 2019, on purpose, at home, surrounded, with a lot of people watching him do it well.","name":"ram dass","where":"be here now for the beginning. still here for after the stroke. his talks are collected by the love serve remember foundation."},"rohr":{"lines":"a franciscan friar, which means a catholic priest in the order started by francis of assisi, the one who talked to birds and gave everything away.\nrohr has spent fifty years in new mexico running a place called the center for action and contemplation, teaching a version of christianity that's more interested in what happens inside you than in who's in and who's out.\nhe writes a lot. falling upward, the universal christ, everything belongs. he's the one who gave a lot of people permission to stay in the tradition without swallowing all of it.\nhe doesn't get everybody all the way there. but he gets you out of the parking lot.","name":"richard rohr","where":"falling upward, 2011. the introduction and the first two chapters carry the whole idea."},"stroke":{"lines":"february 1997. he was sixty five, lying in bed thinking about how he'd write about aging, and a blood vessel in his brain let go. he was found on the floor. paralyzed on the right side, most of his speech gone.\nhe said later that in the moment he looked for the thing he'd taught for thirty years, the witness, the calm center, and it wasn't there. he was just a scared old man on a floor. that was the first lesson.\nhe spent years learning to talk again. slowly. long pauses. his talks after the stroke are half silence, and people say those were his best ones, because he was no longer performing.\nfierce grace was the phrase he landed on. grace that doesn't ask, doesn't soften, and doesn't leave.","name":"the stroke","where":"still here, 2000, is the book he was writing when it happened, finished afterward. fierce grace, 2001, is the documentary. watch the film."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"ram dass, still here · read the book, then find the film fierce grace and watch him try to talk","sourceRef":"stroke","text":"[[ramdass|ram dass]] had a [[stroke|stroke]] in 1997.\nit took his right side and most of his words. he called it fierce grace. not at first. at first he called it what you'd call it.\nbut the stroke did what forty years of meditating hadn't. it took away the guy who was good at talking about god, and left the one who had to sit there."},"theme":"what the break is for","id":"2026-09-23"},{"caption":"the river won't stay in the frame. the bush won't burn up.","date":"2026-09-24","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g24' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--sage)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g24)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M0 270 q 60 -18 120 0 t 120 0 t 120 0 t 40 0' opacity='.55'/><path d='M20 292 q 60 -14 120 0 t 120 0 t 120 0 t 20 0' opacity='.3'/><circle cx='150' cy='120' r='15.0'/><path d='M150 135.0 L 150 169.0'/><path d='M150 145.0 L 136.0 129.0 M150 145.0 L 164.0 129.0'/><path d='M150 169.0 L 138.0 209.0 M150 169.0 L 162.0 209.0'/><rect x='100' y='70' width='100' height='70' rx='2'/><path d='M560 270 q -20 -40 10 -50 q 10 -30 40 -20 q 30 -20 40 10 q 30 0 10 30 q 10 30 -20 30 z'/><path d='M575 232 q 6 -20 -4 -30 q 14 4 12 22 M600 215 q 10 -22 -2 -34 q 18 6 14 26 M625 226 q 8 -18 -2 -28 q 14 6 10 22' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.8'/><circle cx='690' cy='140' r='15.0'/><path d='M690 155.0 L 690 189.0'/><path d='M690 165.0 L 674.0 183.0 M690 165.0 L 706.0 183.0'/><path d='M690 189.0 L 678.0 229.0 M690 189.0 L 702.0 229.0'/><path d='M724 268 q 8 -4 16 0 M746 270 q 8 -4 16 0' stroke-width='2.5' opacity='.7'/><path d='M400 270 L 800 270' opacity='.55'/><\/g><g transform='translate(590,209.65) scale(1.05)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 1 · read 1 through 4 in a row, any translation","sourceRef":"tao","text":"the second you name it, you missed it.\nfirst line of the [[tao|tao te ching]], about twenty five hundred years old: the way you can talk about isn't the way.\n[[laotzu|lao tzu]] opens his whole book by telling you the book can't do it. then he writes eighty more chapters anyway. that's the honest version of every scripture."},"mode":"agree","question":"what have you named that never told you its name?","refs":{"bush":{"lines":"moses is out with his father in law's sheep on a mountain called horeb, and a bush is on fire and not burning up. he goes over to look, which is the whole thing. he could have kept walking.\nthe voice tells him to take his sandals off, the ground is holy. then it tells him it has seen the misery of his people in egypt and it's sending him.\nwhen he asks for a name, the answer in hebrew is ehyeh asher ehyeh. i am who i am, or i will be what i will be. the verb is unfinished on purpose.\nfrom that answer the jewish tradition gets the four letter name it doesn't say out loud, and christians and muslims inherit the same refusal to pin the thing down. every argument about god's name starts at this bush.","name":"the burning bush","where":"exodus 3, the first fifteen verses, slowly."},"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible. in hebrew it's called shemot, which means names, after its first line.\nit's the story of a people enslaved in egypt, the plagues, the escape through the sea, the mountain, the ten commandments, and a lot of instructions about building a tent.\nchapter 3 is where the whole thing turns. an old shepherd, a bush, a name that isn't one. everything after runs on that conversation.","name":"exodus","where":"exodus, chapter 3 today. chapters 1 through 20 for the story."},"laotzu":{"lines":"the name just means old master. nobody is sure he existed as one person.\nthe story goes that he kept the archives at the royal court, got tired of the whole thing, and rode west on a water buffalo to leave the country. the gatekeeper at the border recognized him and wouldn't let him through until he wrote down what he knew.\nhe wrote five thousand characters, handed them over, and rode off. nobody saw him again. that's the book.\nwhether it happened or not, it's the right story for a book that begins by saying words won't get you there.","name":"lao tzu","where":"sima qian's records of the grand historian, written around 100 bc, has the oldest version of the story. it's already a legend by then."},"moses":{"lines":"a hebrew baby raised in the egyptian palace after his mother floated him down the nile to save him from a killing order. he grew up a prince, killed an egyptian he saw beating a slave, and ran.\nhe spent forty years in the desert as a shepherd, married, raising kids, done with all of it. he was eighty when the bush caught fire.\nhe argued with the voice for two chapters. who am i. what's your name. they won't believe me. i can't talk right. send somebody else. every excuse got answered and he went.\nhe led the slaves out of egypt, spent forty more years in the desert with them, and died on a mountain looking at the land he never got to enter. the five books that open the bible are traditionally his.","name":"moses","where":"exodus, chapters 2 through 4 for the beginning. deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"tao":{"lines":"eighty one short chapters, about five thousand chinese characters in all. you can read the whole thing in an hour and spend the rest of your life on it.\ntao means the way. te means the power or virtue that comes from being lined up with it. ching means classic. so, the classic of the way and its power.\nit's the root text of taoism and it leaks into everything else in china, including zen, which is what you get when buddhism moves in next door to this book.\nit doesn't tell you what the way is. it says it can't. then it spends eighty chapters pointing: water, valleys, empty bowls, the space inside a wheel. the useful part is always the part that isn't there.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4 to start. stephen mitchell's version reads easiest, ursula le guin's is the most honest about being a guess."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 3:14 · read all of chapter 3, and notice what moses tries to get out of","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"[[moses]] asked for a name.\nhe's barefoot in front of a [[bush|bush that's on fire and not burning up]], and he asks, who do i say sent me.\nthe answer is i am who i am. that's not a name. that's a refusal to be one.\nwe've spent three thousand years painting a face on it anyway."},"theme":"the name it never gave","id":"2026-09-24"},{"caption":"the strings of the lute are the lanes he runs in.","date":"2026-09-25","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='g25' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--blue)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#g25)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M20 250 L 380 250 M20 282 L 380 282' opacity='.45'/><path d='M30 300 L 380 300' opacity='.25'/><circle cx='230' cy='150' r='15.0'/><path d='M230 165.0 L 230 199.0'/><path d='M230 175.0 L 212.0 169.0 M230 175.0 L 248.0 185.0'/><path d='M230 199.0 L 204.0 229.0 L 196.0 241.0 M230 199.0 L 248.0 225.0 L 240.0 243.0'/><path d='M150 262 q 10 -6 20 0 M120 270 q 10 -6 20 0 M170 276 q 8 -5 16 0' stroke-width='2' opacity='.5'/><path d='M40 236 L 70 236 L 76 250 L 34 250 Z'/><circle cx='560' cy='150' r='15.0'/><path d='M560 165.0 L 560 199.0'/><path d='M560 175.0 L 544.0 193.0 M560 175.0 L 576.0 193.0'/><path d='M560 199.0 L 530.0 209.0 L 556.0 217.0 M560 199.0 L 590.0 209.0 L 564.0 217.0'/><ellipse cx='612' cy='214' rx='22' ry='16'/><path d='M632 206 L 700 176'/><path d='M612 214 L 400 250 M612 214 L 400 282 M612 214 L 400 300' stroke-width='2' opacity='.55'/><\/g><g transform='translate(42,209.0) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"1 corinthians 9:24-27 · read the whole chapter, he's answering a complaint","sourceRef":"corinthians","text":"run like you mean it.\n[[paul]] told the [[corinthians]] every runner runs but only one gets the prize, so run like that one.\nthen he says he beats his body and makes it his slave. that's the actual language. the man was not into balance."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which string are you tuning, and which one are you just yanking on?","refs":{"buddha":{"lines":"siddhartha gautama, a prince from a small kingdom on the edge of what's now nepal, about twenty five hundred years ago. raised behind palace walls, kept from seeing sickness, old age, or death. when he finally saw them, he left. wife, kid, everything, at twenty nine.\nfor six years he did the hardest practices he could find, starving himself down to skin over bone. it didn't work. he ate a meal, sat down under a tree, and woke up. that's what buddha means, the one who woke up.\nthe first thing he taught after that was the middle way. not the palace, not the starving. the lute string, before he ever said it to sona.\nhe taught for forty five years and died at eighty, lying between two trees, telling his monks to work out their own freedom.","name":"the buddha","where":"the pali canon, the oldest collection of his talks. for his own story, the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26."},"corinthians":{"lines":"corinth was a port city in greece, rich, rough, and famous for it. paul started a church there around the year 50 and stayed about a year and a half.\nafter he left, it fell apart in every direction. factions, lawsuits between members, a man sleeping with his stepmother, rich people eating the shared meal before the poor arrived, arguments about who had the better spiritual gifts.\nfirst corinthians is paul's answer to all of it. it's also where the famous love chapter lives, chapter 13, which people read at weddings without knowing it was written to people who couldn't stand each other.\nchapter 9 is him defending himself against members who questioned whether he was a real apostle. the runner line comes at the end of that argument.","name":"the corinthians","where":"1 corinthians, all sixteen chapters. read 9 for today, then 13 to see what he was actually after."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe used athletes as a picture of the spiritual life more than once. corinth hosted the isthmian games, second only to the olympics, so his readers had seen the runners.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. 1 corinthians 9 for the runners. 2 corinthians 11 for what it cost him."},"sona":{"lines":"sona kolivisa, a rich young man from a merchant family who became a monk under the buddha. before that he'd been a musician, a lute player.\nhe threw himself at the practice the way he'd probably thrown himself at everything. he did walking meditation until the soles of his feet split open and the path was bloody, and he still wasn't free. he started thinking about quitting and going back to his money.\nthe buddha came to find him. instead of a lecture he asked about the lute. that conversation is the whole sutta.\nsona stayed, and the tradition says he got there. the lute simile is one of the most quoted things the buddha ever said about effort.","name":"sona","where":"the sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55. it also shows up in the vinaya, the monks' rulebook, mahavagga 5."},"sutta":{"lines":"a sutta is one talk, or one conversation, that the buddha or one of his students gave. thousands of them were memorized and chanted for a few hundred years before anybody wrote them down.\nthe collection is the pali canon, named for the language it's in. it's sorted into groups. the anguttara nikaya is the numbered collection, where talks are grouped by how many things they list. the sona sutta lives in the book of sixes, which is why it's 6.55.\nnone of it is scripture in the sense of a rulebook handed down from the sky. it's a record of a man talking to people about how to suffer less. some of it is dry. some of it, like sona and his lute, is a story you can hold.","name":"what a sutta is","where":"anguttara nikaya, the book of sixes, sutta 55. any translation of the pali canon."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"sona sutta, anguttara nikaya 6.55 · read the whole conversation, it's short","sourceRef":"sutta","text":"loosen the string.\na monk named [[sona]] walked until his feet bled and still wasn't getting anywhere.\n[[buddha|the buddha]] didn't tell him to try harder. he asked about the lute sona used to play. too tight and the string snaps. too loose and there's no note.\ntune it. then play."},"theme":"how hard to push","id":"2026-09-25"},{"caption":"the light in the cell and the light at the grave are the same light.","date":"2026-09-26","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' aria-label='a figure in a cell lit by a tiny window on the left, a small crowd at a graveside on the right, one light crossing both'><defs><linearGradient id='beam2' x1='0' y1='0' x2='0' y2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.7'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.15'/><\/linearGradient><radialGradient id='glow2' cx='.5' cy='1' r='.9'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.55'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><\/radialGradient><linearGradient id='band2' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><stop offset='.5' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='.35'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='#ffffff' stop-opacity='0'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='420' y='0' width='380' height='320' fill='url(#glow2)'/><polygon points='268,70 292,70 360,290 60,290' fill='url(#beam2)'/><rect x='300' y='150' width='240' height='90' fill='url(#band2)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M40 40 L 40 290 L 340 290 L 340 40 Z' opacity='.85'/><rect x='268' y='60' width='24' height='20'/><path d='M280 60 L 280 80 M268 70 L 292 70' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='190' cy='166' r='16'/><path d='M190 182 L 190 236'/><path d='M190 196 L 166 214 M190 196 L 214 214'/><path d='M190 236 L 172 288 M190 236 L 208 288'/><path d='M450 288 Q 640 280 780 288' opacity='.5'/><path d='M560 288 q 40 -30 80 0' opacity='.8'/><path d='M600 258 L 600 240 M590 248 L 610 248' stroke-width='2.5'/><g stroke-width='2.5'><circle cx='500' cy='206' r='11'/><path d='M500 217 L 500 258 M500 228 L 488 244 M500 228 L 512 244 M500 258 L 490 288 M500 258 L 510 288'/><circle cx='535' cy='200' r='11'/><path d='M535 211 L 535 254 M535 222 L 524 238 M535 222 L 546 238 M535 254 L 526 288 M535 254 L 544 288'/><circle cx='680' cy='204' r='11'/><path d='M680 215 L 680 256 M680 226 L 668 240 M680 226 L 692 240 M680 256 L 670 288 M680 256 L 690 288'/><circle cx='712' cy='210' r='11'/><path d='M712 221 L 712 258 M712 232 L 702 246 M712 232 L 722 246 M712 258 L 704 288 M712 258 L 720 288'/><\/g><circle cx='760' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M760 164 L 760 226'/><path d='M760 178 L 744 200 M760 178 L 776 200'/><path d='M760 226 L 748 288 M760 226 L 772 288'/><\/g><g transform='translate(96,9) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"philippians 4:4 · read the whole letter, it's short, and notice where he's writing from","sourceRef":"chains","text":"rejoice. and again i say it.\n[[paul]] wrote that [[chains|in chains]]. not from a beach. he said it twice in one sentence like he knew you'd argue.\nhappiness isn't a report on your circumstances. it's a decision you make with your circumstances still standing there."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one do you reach for because it's true, and which one because it's easier?","refs":{"chains":{"lines":"philippians is one of the prison letters. in chapter 1 he says his chains have become known through the whole palace guard, and that being locked up has actually spread the message further, so he's fine with it.\nscholars argue about which prison, rome or ephesus or caesarea. doesn't matter for our purposes. the man who wrote rejoice always was writing it from a cell, to people who were worried about him, and he was the one doing the comforting.\nsame letter, chapter 4, a few lines after the one we used: he says he has learned to be content with plenty and with nothing, well fed and hungry. he wasn't guessing.","name":"in chains","where":"philippians, all four chapters. it takes about fifteen minutes."},"ecclesiastes":{"lines":"the writer calls himself qoheleth, the teacher, or the one who gathers people. son of david, king in jerusalem, which is why tradition says solomon.\nhe tried everything. wisdom, pleasure, wine, building projects, gardens, wealth, women, work. and he calls all of it hevel, a hebrew word that means vapor or breath. something you can see for a second and can't hold. most bibles translate it vanity, which is weaker.\nthe book almost didn't make it in. the rabbis argued over it for a long time. what saved it was the last few verses, fear god and keep the commandments, and plenty of people think somebody nervous added those.\nread it either way. it's the most honest book in there about how little of your life you actually control.","name":"ecclesiastes","where":"ecclesiastes, twelve chapters. start at 1 and don't skip to the ending."},"mourning":{"lines":"chapter 7 opens with a run of better-than sayings. a good name is better than fine oil. the day of death is better than the day of birth. and then this one: better to go to the house of mourning than the house of feasting, because death is where everyone ends up and the living should take it to heart.\nhe keeps going. sorrow is better than laughter. a sad face is good for the heart. the heart of the wise is in the house of mourning, the heart of fools in the house of pleasure.\nthe house of mourning is a funeral, a shiva, the week after. the house of feasting is the party. he isn't against parties. he's against forgetting.","name":"the house of mourning","where":"ecclesiastes 7, the first four verses, then keep reading."},"paul":{"lines":"born saul, a pharisee from tarsus, a tentmaker by trade. he was on the road to damascus to arrest christians when a light knocked him flat and a voice asked why he was doing it. he was blind for three days.\nthen he spent the rest of his life starting small churches around the mediterranean and writing them letters when they got into trouble, which was constantly. thirteen letters in the new testament carry his name.\nhe was beaten, shipwrecked, stoned once and left for dead outside a town, and jailed more than once. tradition says rome finally executed him.\nhe's also the one who stood up in athens and quoted their own greek poets back at them, in acts 17, to tell them the unknown god they'd built an altar to had a name. old texts talking to each other. he did it first.","name":"paul","where":"acts 9 for the road. acts 17 for athens. 2 corinthians 11 for the list of what it cost him, in his own words."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"ecclesiastes 7:2-4 · read the whole chapter, it doesn't get easier","sourceRef":"mourning","text":"go to the funeral.\n[[ecclesiastes]] says it flat out. the [[mourning|house of mourning]] beats the house of feasting, and the wise keep their heart in the first one.\nnot because sadness is holy. because the party lets you forget you're going to die, and forgetting makes you stupid about what matters."},"theme":"the party or the funeral","id":"2026-09-26"},{"caption":"the road only goes as far as the fence. past that, nothing's been drawn yet.","date":"2026-09-27","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' aria-label='a figure sitting by a cart on the left, a road that stops, a figure climbing a fence at night on the right'><defs><linearGradient id='fold1' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><linearGradient id='fade1' x1='0' x2='1'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)' stop-opacity='0'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--paper)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--marigold)'/><rect x='400' y='0' width='400' height='320' fill='var(--peach)'/><rect x='340' y='0' width='120' height='320' fill='url(#fold1)'/><rect x='690' y='0' width='110' height='320' fill='url(#fade1)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M30 250 Q 120 244 200 250 T 330 248' opacity='.5'/><rect x='58' y='196' width='70' height='40' rx='4'/><circle cx='74' cy='250' r='12'/><circle cx='114' cy='250' r='12'/><path d='M128 206 L 152 190'/><circle cx='210' cy='186' r='16'/><path d='M210 202 L 210 236'/><path d='M210 212 L 190 226 M210 212 L 232 222'/><path d='M210 236 L 178 246 L 206 254 M210 236 L 242 246 L 214 254'/><path d='M150 262 L 300 262' stroke-dasharray='14 12' opacity='.85'/><path d='M300 262 L 330 262' stroke-dasharray='4 8' opacity='.5'/><path d='M334 254 L 334 270' opacity='.5'/><path d='M400 262 L 560 262' stroke-dasharray='14 12' opacity='.85'/><path d='M570 208 L 570 272 M690 208 L 690 272 M560 224 L 700 224 M560 248 L 700 248'/><circle cx='612' cy='158' r='16'/><path d='M612 174 L 612 216'/><path d='M612 186 L 588 198 M612 186 L 636 176'/><path d='M612 216 L 596 254 M612 216 L 652 238 L 660 258'/><path d='M742 60 a 20 20 0 1 0 8 36 a 16 16 0 0 1 -8 -36' opacity='.75'/><path d='M660 62 l 4 0 M700 100 l 4 0 M770 130 l 4 0 M630 40 l 4 0' stroke-width='4' opacity='.7'/><\/g><path d='M186 226 q 10 -50 44 -48' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='2' stroke-dasharray='2 6' opacity='.5'/><g transform='translate(676,176) scale(1.15)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><g transform='translate(70,204) scale(1.5)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"bhagavad gita 2:47 · read all of chapter 2, not the verse","sourceRef":"gita","text":"you don't get the ending.\n[[arjuna]] sat down in the chariot and wouldn't fight because he could already see how it goes. [[krishna]] didn't argue with the forecast. he told him the results were never his to begin with.\nyou get the work. that's the whole deal. the fruit goes somewhere you don't control."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you carrying that hasn't happened yet?","refs":{"arjuna":{"lines":"the best archer alive, third of five brothers, and the one the whole gita is talking to.\nthe war is a family fight over a kingdom. his cousins are on the other side. so is bhishma, the great-uncle who helped raise him, and drona, the man who taught him to shoot.\nin chapter one he asks krishna to drive the chariot out between the two armies so he can look. he looks. his hands go weak, the bow slides out, and he sits down on the floor of the chariot and says he won't fight.\nnobody in the story treats that as cowardice. krishna just doesn't accept it as the end of the conversation. the other seventeen chapters are what gets said while arjuna is sitting there.","name":"arjuna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 1, which sits inside the mahabharata. any translation. the old edwin arnold one is free everywhere."},"gita":{"lines":"seven hundred verses tucked into the mahabharata, the enormous indian war epic, roughly ten times the length of the iliad and the odyssey put together.\neighteen chapters, all one conversation, held on a battlefield in the minute before the fighting starts. hindus read it as scripture. gandhi read it most mornings. ram dass's whole path runs through it.\nchapter 2 is where krishna starts answering. 2:47 is the verse we used: you have a right to your work, never to its fruits. read the whole chapter and you'll see he's not being gentle about it.","name":"the bhagavad gita","where":"bhagavad gita, chapter 2. any translation you can hold in your hand."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher from a small town in galilee, about two thousand years ago. he spent roughly three years walking around telling stories, eating with the wrong people, and healing whoever showed up. the romans executed him.\nchristians say he got up again three days later and that he was god the whole time. you don't have to sign any of that to read him.\nfour short books tell his story: matthew, mark, luke, and john. they don't fully agree with each other on the details, which is part of the honesty of the thing.\nthis book reads him the way a mystic would. less rule-giver, more the guy pointing at the birds and saying look.","name":"jesus","where":"the gospels: matthew, mark, luke, john. start with mark, it's the shortest and the fastest."},"krishna":{"lines":"arjuna's charioteer, friend, and cousin on his mother's side. he agreed to drive but not to fight.\nsomewhere in chapter two it becomes clear he is also god, and in chapter eleven arjuna asks to see that directly and immediately wishes he hadn't. krishna puts the friendly face back on and they keep talking.\nhe never once tells arjuna the fight will go well. he tells him whose the outcome is, and it isn't arjuna's. do the work, hand over the result. that's the line we used, and it's probably the most quoted line in the book.","name":"krishna","where":"bhagavad gita, chapters 2 and 11 for the two faces of him."},"sermon":{"lines":"matthew 5, 6, and 7. jesus goes up a hill, sits down, and talks for three chapters straight.\nit opens with the beatitudes, blessed are the poor in spirit, the ones who mourn, the meek. then anger, lust, oaths, revenge, loving your enemies, giving in secret, the lord's prayer, money, worry, judging other people, and it ends with a house built on rock and one built on sand.\nthe worry part is the end of chapter 6. look at the birds, look at the lilies, they aren't working themselves to death and they're fine. then the line we used. people cut that line into posters. read straight through, it's a lot harder than the posters, because he's just told you to give away your coat and love the people who hit you.","name":"the sermon on the mount","where":"matthew, chapters 5 through 7. any bible, any translation, one sitting."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"matthew 6:34 · read the whole sermon, chapters 5 through 7","sourceRef":"sermon","text":"you don't get tomorrow either.\n[[jesus]] said [[sermon|don't worry about it]], tomorrow has its own problems, today has enough.\npeople read that as comfort. it isn't. it's a fence. you're not allowed past midnight, and you keep climbing it at 2am anyway."},"theme":"what's yours to carry","id":"2026-09-27"},{"caption":"what one of them shouts is the smoke the other lets burn off.","date":"2026-09-28","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'> <defs> <linearGradient id='sf20260928bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'> <stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/> <stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/> <stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/> <stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/> <\/linearGradient> <\/defs> <rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260928bg)' stroke='none'/> <path d='M 0 285 C 40 275 80 295 120 285 S 200 275 240 285 S 320 295 360 285 S 420 280 460 283 L 770 283'/> <path d='M 90 262 C 88 220 92 180 95 130'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 120 118 150 138 160 205'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 70 116 42 140 46 205'/> <path d='M 95 130 C 108 128 128 150 132 222'/> <path d='M 96 138 C 115 145 130 160 158 168'/> <path d='M 46 190 L 46 216 M 58 190 L 58 216 M 46 190 Q 52 180 58 190 M 50 194 L 50 214 M 54 194 L 54 214'/> <circle cx='250' cy='150' r='14'/> <ellipse cx='257' cy='155' rx='3' ry='4'/> <path d='M 250 164 L 250 220 M 250 220 L 235 260 M 250 220 L 265 260 M 250 182 L 226 150 M 250 182 L 276 158'/> <path d='M 278 138 L 288 132 M 281 150 L 293 150'/> <path d='M 264 146 C 300 128 320 170 355 145 C 385 122 400 142 420 118 C 445 92 480 95 500 125 C 520 155 545 190 555 220 C 560 236 566 244 565 252'/> <path d='M 548 283 L 582 283'/> <path d='M 555 283 C 549 266 568 262 565 252 C 573 262 581 270 578 283'/> <circle cx='540' cy='287' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'/> <circle cx='591' cy='286' r='2' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='none' opacity='.5'/> <circle cx='660' cy='205' r='14'/> <path d='M 655 211 L 665 211'/> <path d='M 660 219 L 660 262 M 660 262 C 640 262 630 280 645 283 M 660 262 C 680 262 690 280 675 283 M 660 236 L 646 262 M 660 236 L 674 262'/> <g transform='translate(128,135) scale(1.1)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g> <\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"psalm 137 · read the whole thing, then 88, the one that ends in the dark","sourceRef":"psalm137","text":"say the ugly thing.\nthere's a [[psalms|psalm]] that ends with a man in [[exile]] hoping somebody smashes his enemy's babies on the rocks. [[psalm137|psalm 137]]. they left it in the book.\nyou're allowed to say what you actually feel to god. apparently he's heard worse.\nwhat you swallow doesn't leave. it just moves in."},"mode":"disagree","question":"what are you swallowing right now, and what's it feeding?","refs":{"bodhicaryavatara":{"lines":"shantideva's book. ten chapters, written in verse in the eighth century, on how to become a bodhisattva, which is someone who's waking up not for their own sake but for everybody else's.\nchapter 6 is on patience and it opens with the line about a single moment of anger burning up a thousand eons of good deeds. then it works through every excuse you've got for staying mad, and takes each one apart.\nthe famous image from the book is in chapter 5: you'll never find enough leather to cover the whole earth, but with leather on the soles of your feet it's as if you had. you don't fix the world. you fix where you stand on it.\nfor today, the book is the strongest case in any tradition for not saying the thing. not swallowing it either. just not feeding it.","name":"the way of the bodhisattva","where":"chapter 6, patience. read the first ten verses, then the stretch near the end of the chapter where the enemy becomes the teacher."},"exile":{"lines":"in 586 bc the babylonian army under nebuchadnezzar took jerusalem, burned the temple, and marched the leadership of judah hundreds of miles east to babylon. that's the exile. it lasted about fifty years.\nit was the worst thing that had ever happened to those people. the temple was where god lived, as far as they knew. now it was rubble and they were singing in a foreign country.\na lot of the hebrew bible got its final shape in that stretch, because people were trying to figure out how to be who they were with nothing left. then the persian king cyrus took babylon and let them go home.\npsalm 137 was written inside that wound. the anger in it isn't a thought experiment. it's people who watched their city die.","name":"the exile","where":"2 kings 24 and 25 tell the fall. lamentations is the grief. psalm 137 is the anger. ezra 1 is the trip home."},"patience":{"lines":"one of the six perfections in mahayana buddhism, the qualities a bodhisattva practices: generosity, discipline, patience, effort, meditation, wisdom. the sanskrit word is kshanti.\nit doesn't mean gritting your teeth and waiting. it means not getting knocked off center by what hurts you, and not making it worse. shantideva says there's no austerity like it, no practice that does more.\nthe trick in his version is that you can't practice it in a nice room. it only exists in the presence of somebody who's pissing you off. that's why he calls the enemy a treasure found lying in your house.\nfor today: the right page isn't telling you to be nice. it's telling you the person you want to say the ugly thing to is your practice, and the fire goes out on its own if you stop feeding it.","name":"patience (kshanti)","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6, all of it."},"psalm137":{"lines":"a song written by people who'd just lost everything. jerusalem burned, the temple gone, and them marched off to babylon. it opens with the line most people know: by the rivers of babylon we sat down and wept. their captors ask them to sing one of the old songs for fun, and they hang their harps in the trees and refuse.\nthen the last two verses turn. the singer asks god to remember what was done to his city, and says whoever grabs babylon's babies and smashes them on the rocks will be happy. that's the end of the psalm. no apology, no take-back.\nnobody edited it out. it's been in the prayer book of jews and christians for twenty five hundred years, and monks still chant it. scholars call these the imprecatory psalms, the cursing ones, and there are more of them than you'd think.\nit matters today because the book makes room for rage. not tidy rage. the real kind. and it puts that rage in front of god instead of behind your teeth.","name":"psalm 137","where":"the book of psalms, psalm 137. then read psalm 88, the only one that ends without a way out."},"psalms":{"lines":"a hundred and fifty songs and poems sitting in the middle of the bible. they were the hymnbook of ancient israel, and later the prayer book of jesus, of paul, of the monks, of pretty much everyone since.\na lot of them are credited to king david, though not all, and plenty are older or later. they were meant to be sung, not read quietly.\nabout a third of them are laments. complaints, really. why have you left me, how long, where are you. the praise ones get quoted on coffee mugs. the lament ones are where the honesty lives.\nfor today: the psalms are proof that saying the worst thing out loud to god was normal, expected, and written down for everyone to sing.","name":"the psalms","where":"the book of psalms, dead center of the bible. try 23, 51, 88, and 137 back to back to get the range."},"shantideva":{"lines":"a buddhist monk in india in the eighth century, living at nalanda, the great monastery and university of the day. the story goes the other monks thought he was useless. he seemed to do nothing but eat, sleep, and go to the toilet, and they gave him a nickname that meant exactly that.\nso they set him up. they asked him to recite scripture in front of everyone, figuring he'd have nothing. he asked if they wanted something old or something new. they said new. he sat down and recited the whole of the way of the bodhisattva from memory, and the legend says partway through the wisdom chapter he rose into the air and was gone.\nwhether that happened or not, the book is real and it's the one tibetan teachers reach for first when they want to talk about how to actually live this stuff. the dalai lama teaches from it constantly.\nhis chapter on patience is the hardest thing in it. he doesn't argue anger is bad. he argues it's expensive, and that the people who hurt you are the only ones who ever give you a place to practice.","name":"shantideva","where":"the way of the bodhisattva, also called the bodhicaryavatara. the padmakara translation reads clean. the dalai lama's book healing anger is a commentary on chapter 6."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"shantideva, the way of the bodhisattva, chapter 6 · read the whole chapter, it's on patience","sourceRef":"bodhicaryavatara","text":"don't feed it.\n[[shantideva]] was a monk in india in the eighth century, and he said one flash of anger burns up a thousand lifetimes of good. everything you built. one match.\nhe also said the guy who wronged you is the only one who ever hands you a chance at [[patience]]. so quit trying to get rid of him.\nanger needs a mouth to stay alive. close it."},"theme":"say it or starve it","id":"2026-09-28"},{"caption":"what one of them left alone is growing. what the other one kept is rotting.","date":"2026-09-29","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260929bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--clay)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260929bg)' stroke='none'/><path d='M 0 272 C 60 262 110 280 170 272 S 290 262 340 270 C 370 274 390 272 410 272 L 790 270'/><path d='M 60 248 L 68 272 L 118 272 L 126 248 Z'/><path d='M 66 248 C 80 218 106 218 120 248'/><circle cx='86' cy='266' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='101' cy='267' r='4' fill='var(--peach)'/><circle cx='176' cy='158' r='14'/><path d='M 181 164 L 190 168'/><path d='M 178 172 C 181 190 183 206 184 222'/><path d='M 184 222 L 170 268 L 140 268'/><path d='M 184 222 L 214 248 L 214 268'/><path d='M 181 186 L 208 236'/><path d='M 181 186 C 200 194 218 200 232 212'/><path d='M 232 212 L 238 206 M 232 212 L 240 212 M 232 212 L 237 218'/><path d='M 262 270 C 262 258 262 250 264 240'/><path d='M 263 250 C 253 250 247 242 249 236 C 255 238 261 244 263 250'/><path d='M 263 248 C 273 248 279 240 277 234 C 271 236 265 242 263 248'/><circle cx='265' cy='237' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 305 270 C 305 258 305 250 307 240'/><path d='M 306 250 C 296 250 290 242 292 236 C 298 238 304 244 306 250'/><path d='M 306 248 C 316 248 322 240 320 234 C 314 236 308 242 306 248'/><circle cx='308' cy='237' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 348 271 C 348 259 348 251 350 241'/><path d='M 349 251 C 339 251 333 243 335 237 C 341 239 347 245 349 251'/><path d='M 349 249 C 359 249 365 241 363 235 C 357 237 351 243 349 249'/><circle cx='351' cy='238' r='5' fill='var(--peach)'/><path d='M 400 272 C 400 264 400 260 401 250'/><path d='M 401 258 C 394 258 389 252 391 247 C 396 249 400 253 401 258'/><path d='M 401 257 C 408 257 413 251 411 246 C 406 248 402 252 401 257'/><circle cx='402' cy='249' r='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><g fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none'><circle cx='372' cy='266' r='2'/><circle cx='386' cy='268' r='2'/><circle cx='414' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='426' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='440' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='452' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='466' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='534' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='548' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='562' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='700' cy='266' r='2.5'/><circle cx='716' cy='268' r='2.5'/><circle cx='732' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='748' cy='267' r='2.5'/><circle cx='764' cy='265' r='2.5'/><circle cx='480' cy='262' r='2.5'/><circle cx='690' cy='262' r='2.5'/><\/g><circle cx='730' cy='84' r='22'/><path d='M 730 50 L 730 42 M 764 84 L 772 84 M 754 60 L 760 54 M 706 60 L 700 54'/><path d='M 560 270 L 620 182 L 680 270'/><path d='M 620 182 L 620 270'/><path d='M 600 270 L 620 236 L 640 270'/><circle cx='498' cy='166' r='14'/><path d='M 505 170 L 511 172'/><path d='M 498 180 L 498 234 M 498 234 L 484 270 M 498 234 L 512 270'/><path d='M 498 196 L 530 206 M 498 196 L 528 214'/><path d='M 532 202 L 528 224 L 552 224 L 548 202 Z'/><path d='M 530 202 L 550 202'/><path d='M 536 196 C 530 186 542 178 536 168 C 530 158 540 150 534 140'/><path d='M 544 196 C 552 184 540 176 548 164 C 556 152 544 144 550 132'/><path d='M 540 192 C 534 178 552 170 542 156 C 532 142 556 132 540 120 C 524 108 500 108 480 112 C 458 116 440 124 424 128'/><g transform='translate(608,155) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"native","source":"robin wall kimmerer, braiding sweetgrass · read the honorable harvest, then the gift of strawberries","sourceRef":"sweetgrass","text":"leave the first one.\n[[kimmerer|robin wall kimmerer]] is a botanist and a member of the [[potawatomi|citizen potawatomi nation]], and the rules she was raised on for taking anything from the ground start like this: ask first. never take the first one you see. it might be the only one.\nthen take only what you need, only what's given, and say thank you like you mean it. the [[harvest|honorable harvest]], she calls it. none of it is written down. you learn it by watching somebody do it.\nyou were taught to grab what you want before somebody else does. the plants have been here longer than you. you're a guest at their table."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you keeping that was only ever given for today?","refs":{"exodus":{"lines":"the second book of the bible, and the story of the israelites getting out of egypt. the name is greek for the way out.\nthe shape: chapters 1 through 15 are the escape, plagues, the sea. chapter 16 is the manna, three days after the singing on the far shore, when the food ran out and everyone wished they were back in egypt. 17 is water from a rock. 19 through 24 is mount sinai and the ten commandments. the rest is instructions for building a tent for god to live in.\nit reads fast until the tent. the manna chapter is one of the ones that stays with people: the bread is free, the only rule is don't hoard it, and they hoard it.\nfor today, read the whole of 16 and notice the pattern. complaint, gift, rule, broken rule, and god still shows up the next morning.","name":"exodus","where":"the book of exodus, chapter 16. then 15:22 to 27 for the complaint right before it."},"harvest":{"lines":"kimmerer's name for the rules her elders taught about taking anything from the living world: plants, animals, water, wood. she says they aren't written down anywhere, they're passed by story and by example, and she writes them out in the chapter with that title.\nthe gist: know the ones who take care of you. introduce yourself. ask permission before you take, and abide by the answer. never take the first one. never take the last. take only what you need, and only what's given. leave some for others. use it with respect. never waste it. share. give thanks. give something back. sustain the ones who sustain you.\nshe puts it next to the way a market works, where the point of taking is owning, and there's no such thing as enough. she doesn't pretend she's above that either. she writes about walking into a store and trying to hold the same rules there.\nfor today, the first rule is the one that stings. never take the first one you see. you'll want to. that's the point.","name":"the honorable harvest","where":"braiding sweetgrass, the chapter called the honorable harvest."},"israelites":{"lines":"the people of the hebrew bible, descended in the story from a man named jacob, whose other name was israel. his twelve sons became twelve tribes. they went down to egypt during a famine, stayed, and over generations became slaves there.\nthe exodus is the story of getting out. after that they're the people at the foot of mount sinai, the people in the desert, and eventually the people of the land of israel. the jewish people today trace themselves back through them, and christians read their story as the front half of their own.\nin the desert chapters they are not heroic. they complain about water, about food, about moses, about having left egypt at all. the manna shows up in direct answer to one of those complaints. then they break the one rule about it. that's the whole book in miniature.\nfor today, they're you, the morning after you kept some.","name":"the israelites","where":"genesis 32 for jacob getting the name, exodus 1 for how they ended up enslaved, exodus 16 for the manna and the grumbling."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish teacher in roman occupied galilee about two thousand years ago, executed by the romans, and the person christianity is named after. the four gospels are the record of what he said and did, written down in the decades after.\nhe taught his followers one prayer, in matthew 6. one line of it asks for bread for today, not for the week. read next to the manna it's the same rule: enough for now, don't stockpile. in the same sermon he says don't worry about tomorrow, it has enough trouble of its own.\nhe also had a habit of feeding people. in john 6 he feeds a crowd with a few loaves and afterward tells his people to gather up the leftovers so nothing is wasted. take only what's given, and waste none of it. kimmerer's rules, from the other side of the world.\nfor today, he's the one who took the manna story and turned it into a prayer you can say in five seconds.","name":"jesus","where":"matthew 6 for the prayer and the bit about tomorrow. john 6 for the bread and the manna argument."},"kimmerer":{"lines":"a botanist who teaches at the state university of new york's college of environmental science and forestry in syracuse, and an enrolled member of the citizen potawatomi nation. she's been both of those things her whole career, and for a long time the two didn't talk to each other.\nshe tells the story of showing up to college wanting to study why asters and goldenrod look so good together, and being told by her advisor that wasn't science. it took her years to find her way back to the question. her book braiding sweetgrass, published in 2013, is the answer: plants as teachers, and science and indigenous knowledge as two ways of knowing that need each other.\nthe book sold slowly and then, seven years later, hit the bestseller lists on word of mouth alone. she got a macarthur fellowship in 2022. she also founded the center for native peoples and the environment at her school.\nfor today, she's the one who wrote down the rules for taking, and admitted she's had to learn them slowly, one strawberry at a time.","name":"robin wall kimmerer","where":"braiding sweetgrass, milkweed editions, 2013. start with the gift of strawberries, near the front, then the honorable harvest, further in."},"manna":{"lines":"the bread that showed up on the ground in the desert. exodus 16 says when the morning dew lifted there was a fine flaky stuff on the ground, white, and it tasted like wafers made with honey. the people looked at it and said, what is it. in hebrew that's roughly man hu, and the name stuck.\nthe rules were strict. one measure per person per day. gather in the morning, because it melted when the sun got hot. keep none overnight, except on the sixth day, when you gathered double and it kept through the sabbath. anybody who tried to hoard on a normal day found worms in it by morning.\nthe book says it fed them for forty years and stopped the day they ate the first crop from the promised land. a jar of it was supposed to be kept in the ark of the covenant so later generations would know.\njesus gets asked about it in john 6. people want a sign like manna, and he tells them the manna was never the point. your ancestors ate it and died anyway. that's the mystic's reading: it was never about the bread.","name":"manna","where":"exodus 16, the whole chapter. then numbers 11, where they get sick of it, and john 6, where jesus argues about it."},"moses":{"lines":"the man who walked the israelites out of slavery in egypt, and then spent forty years in the desert with them, mostly getting complained at. he's the central human figure of the hebrew bible, and the first five books are traditionally credited to him.\nhis story: born to slaves, hidden in a basket on the nile, raised in pharaoh's own house. killed an egyptian who was beating a hebrew and ran for the desert. spent forty years herding sheep there. then a bush caught fire and didn't burn up, and a voice sent him back to egypt to get everyone out.\nhe wasn't a natural. he told god he couldn't talk right and to send somebody else. later he lost his temper and hit a rock he was told to speak to, and for that he was told he'd never enter the land. he died on a mountain looking at it from across the river.\nfor today, he's the guy delivering the rule about the manna, and the first to watch people ignore it. he'd already learned in the desert, alone, how little you need in a day.","name":"moses","where":"exodus 2 through 4 for the beginning, exodus 16 for the manna, numbers 20 for the rock, deuteronomy 34 for the end."},"potawatomi":{"lines":"a native nation from the great lakes, originally around what's now michigan, wisconsin, indiana and illinois. they're one of the three fires, alongside the ojibwe and the odawa, related peoples who speak related languages. the potawatomi were the keepers of the fire in that alliance.\nin the 1830s the united states pushed them off their land. one group was marched at gunpoint from indiana to kansas in 1838, and enough people died on the way that the potawatomi still call it the trail of death. later they were moved again, to oklahoma. the citizen potawatomi nation is headquartered in shawnee, oklahoma today.\nkimmerer writes that her grandfather was taken to the carlisle indian school in pennsylvania as a boy, where the point was to strip the language and the ways out of native kids. so the potawatomi she carries came to her partly broken, and she's spent her adult life learning it back, including the language, a few words at a time.\nit matters today because the rules on the left page aren't an idea. they're a people's way of staying alive on land, kept through two removals and a boarding school.","name":"the potawatomi","where":"kimmerer tells her family's part in braiding sweetgrass, in the chapter called learning the grammar of animacy, and in the one on her grandfather."},"sweetgrass":{"lines":"robin wall kimmerer's 2013 book, published by milkweed editions, a small nonprofit press in minneapolis. the full title is braiding sweetgrass: indigenous wisdom, scientific knowledge, and the teachings of plants.\nsweetgrass is a fragrant grass that's sacred to a lot of native nations. it gets braided in three strands, and the book is built the same way: her people's teachings, her training as a scientist, and what the plants themselves have to say. the sections are named for working the grass: planting, tending, picking, braiding, burning.\nit's essays, not a manual. one is about wild strawberries and what a gift is. one is about her potawatomi language teacher and why the language has a word for a bay that's a verb, not a noun. one is the honorable harvest. one is the thanksgiving address the haudenosaunee open their gatherings with, which goes on for a while because it thanks everything.\nthe thread through all of it is reciprocity. the world gives, and the only sane response is to give back. that's the whole left page.","name":"braiding sweetgrass","where":"the book itself. the gift of strawberries is near the front. the honorable harvest is in the section called braiding sweetgrass. read those two and you'll know if you want the rest."},"wilderness":{"lines":"the desert between egypt and the land the israelites had been promised, the sinai peninsula, roughly. the trip should have taken about eleven days. deuteronomy says so, flat out. it took forty years.\nwhy forty years: when they got to the edge of the land they sent scouts in, the scouts came back scared, and the people refused to go. so the whole generation that walked out of egypt died in the desert and their kids went in instead. the wandering wasn't the plan. it was the consequence.\ndeuteronomy 8 looks back on it and says the hunger and the manna were there to teach one thing: a person doesn't live on bread alone. centuries later jesus spends forty days hungry in a desert and quotes that exact line back at the devil. old texts talking to each other.\nfor today, the wilderness is where the manna rule gets learned, because it's the one place nobody could pretend they were feeding themselves.","name":"the wilderness","where":"exodus 15 through 18 for the first stretch, numbers 13 and 14 for the refusal, deuteronomy 8 for the point of it, matthew 4 for jesus quoting it."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"exodus 16 · read the whole chapter, and count how many times they complain","sourceRef":"exodus","text":"it rots by morning.\nthe [[israelites]] were starving in the [[wilderness]] and bread showed up on the ground at dawn. [[manna]]. [[moses]] told them to gather what they'd eat that day and keep none of it.\nsome of them kept some. by morning it was full of worms and it stank.\nover a thousand years later [[jesus]] taught people to pray for bread one day at a time. same lesson. whatever you're holding back for later is doing what the manna did. you just can't smell it yet."},"theme":"only what's given, only today","id":"2026-09-29"},{"caption":"the trail one of them walked alone is the rope the other gets pulled out with.","date":"2026-09-30","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20260930bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20260930bg)' stroke='none'/><circle cx='330' cy='58' r='17' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 Z' fill='var(--paper)' stroke='none' opacity='.45'/><path d='M 0 192 C 70 188 150 196 230 192 S 370 190 430 192 L 612 192 L 616 306 L 712 306 L 716 192 L 800 192'/><path d='M 34 192 C 33 150 35 100 34 48'/><path d='M 28 152 L 40 152 M 28 112 L 40 112 M 28 78 L 40 78'/><path d='M 98 192 C 99 150 97 110 98 70'/><path d='M 92 160 L 104 160 M 92 124 L 104 124 M 92 94 L 104 94'/><path d='M 64 192 L 64 152'/><path d='M 57 152 L 71 152 M 58 174 L 70 174'/><path d='M 34 70 C 44 61 57 61 64 68 C 55 75 43 75 34 70 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 34 122 C 26 113 14 113 7 120 C 15 127 26 127 34 122 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><path d='M 98 104 C 108 95 121 95 128 102 C 119 109 107 109 98 104 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='230' cy='104' r='14'/><path d='M 217 106 L 210 108'/><path d='M 230 118 L 228 160'/><path d='M 228 160 L 211 192 M 228 160 L 246 192'/><path d='M 229 132 L 209 146 M 229 132 L 245 151'/><path d='M 210 124 L 204 192'/><path d='M 258 201 C 300 205 350 203 398 201' stroke-dasharray='1 13'/><path d='M 400 201 C 450 199 520 192 560 177 C 580 169 592 164 604 162 C 616 168 624 184 628 200 C 632 216 638 228 645 236'/><circle cx='580' cy='118' r='14'/><path d='M 580 132 L 582 168'/><path d='M 582 168 L 571 192 M 582 168 L 595 192'/><path d='M 581 144 L 604 160 M 581 150 L 600 167'/><circle cx='676' cy='256' r='14'/><path d='M 676 270 L 676 292'/><path d='M 676 292 L 666 306 M 676 292 L 687 306'/><path d='M 676 275 L 656 262 L 648 240 M 676 275 L 660 271 L 652 247'/><g transform='translate(52,125) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"khaggavisana sutta, sutta nipata 1.3 · read all forty one verses, it takes ten minutes","sourceRef":"rhino","text":"walk alone.\nthere's an old poem in the [[palicanon|pali canon]], forty one verses, and nearly every one ends on the same line: wander alone, like a [[rhino|rhinoceros]].\nit's kept with the [[buddha]]'s teachings, and its reasons aren't gentle. you get close to people, you start needing them, and the needing is where it hurts. it says family tangles you up like a bamboo thicket. be the one shoot that grows clear of it.\nnobody can walk this for you. quit waiting for company."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which one are you hiding in right now, the crowd or the cave?","refs":{"bonhoeffer":{"lines":"a german pastor and theologian, born in 1906, hanged by the nazis in april 1945 at thirty nine. he was one of eight children, had his doctorate at twenty one, and could have had a quiet career writing books.\ninstead he was against hitler in public from the first days. two days after hitler took office he was on the radio warning about him, and the broadcast got cut off. when much of the german church went along with the nazis, he joined the pastors who refused, and ended up training their ministers in secret. in the summer of 1939 he left for new york, where he'd have been safe. he turned around within weeks and sailed home, because he didn't think he'd have the right to help rebuild germany if he sat the war out.\nback home he joined the resistance, with a job in military intelligence as his cover. he was arrested in april 1943 and spent two years locked up. they hanged him at the flossenbürg camp on april 9, 1945, two weeks before american soldiers got there.\nhere's why he's on this page. the man who wrote that you can't do it alone spent his last two years in prison, cut off from every one of those men. what came out of it was letters, and most of the ones in the book made from them were written to one friend, eberhard bethge, who'd been his student at the seminary. the community was gone. he kept one brother, by mail.","name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","where":"life together for the community. letters and papers from prison for the cell. eberhard bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography is the full story, written by the friend who got the letters."},"buddha":{"lines":"siddhattha gotama, born about twenty five hundred years ago near what's now the border of nepal and india. buddha isn't a name. it's a title, and it means the one who woke up.\nthe story as it's handed down: he had a comfortable home, a wife, a son. at twenty nine he left all of it, cut off his hair, and went looking for the end of suffering. he studied under two teachers and outgrew both. he spent years starving himself alongside five companions, and when he quit that and ate a real meal, the five walked out on him in disgust. he was by himself when he sat down under the tree and woke up. he was thirty five.\nthen he did the opposite for forty five years. he went and found those same five and taught them first. he built a community of monks and nuns, and he walked the roads of north india with a crowd around him until he died at eighty.\nso he lived both pages of this spread. he got free alone, and then he never lived alone again. the rhinoceros verses sound like the first half of that life.","name":"the buddha","where":"the ariyapariyesana sutta, majjhima nikaya 26, is his own account of leaving home, the two teachers, the five companions, and waking up. the mahaparinibbana sutta, digha nikaya 16, is the last months."},"finkenwalde":{"lines":"a village near the city of stettin, close to the baltic coast. germany then, poland now. a seminary is a school that trains pastors, and in 1935 the confessing church, the german protestants who wouldn't let the nazi state run their church, needed one of its own. they gave the job to bonhoeffer. he was twenty nine.\nhe ran it like nothing his students had seen. twenty three young men that first term, in a neglected old estate they had to clean out before they could live in it. the day started in silence. then psalms read back and forth, and scripture out loud, morning and night. after breakfast, half an hour alone and quiet over one short passage.\npeople sneered that he'd started a monastery. some of his own students joked about it and found it heavy going. he thought protestants had thrown out something they needed.\nthe state declared the training illegal, and in the fall of 1937 the gestapo closed the place and sealed it. by the end of that year twenty seven of his former students had been arrested. he kept the training going in secret for a couple more years, in a small town and a village further east.\nso the book on this page is about something that lasted a little over two years and was already gone when he wrote it down.","name":"finkenwalde","where":"bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, the chapters on the preachers' seminary. bonhoeffer's own version of what they did there is life together."},"gestapo":{"lines":"the secret police of nazi germany. the name is short for geheime staatspolizei, secret state police. hitler took power in 1933 and ran germany until 1945, and the gestapo was how the state dealt with anyone it decided was an enemy. no court had to sign off. they could lock you up on their own say so.\nit was a much smaller outfit than its reputation. most of its cases started with a tip from an ordinary german, a neighbor reporting a neighbor, and the fear did the rest.\na state that wanted to be the only thing anybody answered to had no use for pastors who wouldn't go along. they got watched, silenced and jailed. bonhoeffer lost his right to teach at the university in 1936, was banned from speaking in public in 1940, and from publishing in 1941.\nfor today: the thing that ended his community wasn't an argument or a falling out. it was police at the door. he knew, when he wrote the book, that having your people around you is not a given.","name":"the gestapo","where":"bethge's biography tells the closing of the seminary. bonhoeffer's own line on community being grace, nothing you can claim, is in the first chapter of life together."},"lifetogether":{"lines":"a short book bonhoeffer wrote in 1938, the year after the gestapo closed finkenwalde. he wrote it in the empty home of his twin sister sabine. her husband's father was jewish, which made the whole family jews in nazi eyes, and that fall they fled germany. bonhoeffer drove them to the swiss border himself.\nit's what he learned living with his students, put on paper. five chapters: community, the day with others, the day alone, ministry, and confession and communion. when he says brother he means any other christian, but you can read it as whoever is actually walking this with you.\nthe first chapter is where today's page comes from. he says it isn't a given that you get to live among your own people. jesus lived in the middle of his enemies. so if you've got people, that's grace, nothing you can claim. then the hard part: you need another person to say the true thing to you, because in your own heart it's shaky, and in his mouth it's sure.\nhe's rough on the dream version too. whoever loves his dream of a community more than the actual community, he says, ends up destroying it, however honest and earnest he is about it.\nthen the chapter on the day alone turns around and argues the left page. whoever can't be alone should beware of community. whoever isn't in community should beware of being alone. he sets the two warnings back to back and won't pick.","name":"life together","where":"life together, the first chapter, community, for the brother's word being stronger than your own. the third, the day alone, for the two warnings. it's short enough to read in an evening."},"palicanon":{"lines":"the oldest complete set of buddhist scriptures still in use. pali is an old indian language, a cousin of sanskrit, and this is the collection kept by the theravada school, the buddhism of sri lanka, thailand and burma.\nnobody wrote it down for centuries. monks memorized it and chanted it together, which is why it repeats itself so much. repeating is how you don't lose a line. it was finally put in writing in sri lanka around the first century bc, on palm leaves.\nit's called the three baskets. one holds the rules for monks and nuns. one holds the teachings, thousands of talks and poems. one holds the later analysis, which reads like a parts manual for the mind.\nthe rhinoceros poem sits in the teachings basket, in a small book called the sutta nipata, and scholars count parts of that book among the earliest layers of the whole thing. read it and you're about as close to the first generation as the record gets.","name":"the pali canon","where":"the sutta nipata is one of the short books in the khuddaka nikaya, the last of the five collections of teachings. thanissaro bhikkhu and bhikkhu bodhi have both put the whole sutta nipata into english."},"rhino":{"lines":"a sutta is a teaching of the buddha, memorized and handed down. most are talks. this one is a poem, forty one verses, called the khaggavisana sutta in pali, the language it was kept in. it's counted among the oldest material in the whole collection. a copy on birch bark from about two thousand years ago, from the old region of gandhara and now in the british library, is among the oldest buddhist manuscripts anyone has.\nthe refrain is the whole poem. wander alone like a rhinoceros. translators argue about it, because the pali word can mean the animal or its horn, and the indian rhino has just the one. either way the picture is the same. one, not two.\nthe verses go after everything you lean on. friends, children, a spouse, good company. then come the pictures of what free looks like. a deer in the woods that eats where it wants. a lion that doesn't jump at noises. wind a net can't hold. a lotus the water doesn't stick to.\nhere's what the hit line leaves out. one spot breaks the pattern. if you find a companion who's wise and steady, walk with him and be glad. if you don't, go alone, like a king walking away from his kingdom. so the poem was never against people. it was against company that makes you smaller.\ntwo more things. the tradition's own old commentary says these verses weren't first spoken by the buddha. it credits them to solitary buddhas, people who woke up on their own and never taught. and the buddha himself, in another sutta, corrects his attendant ananda for saying good friends are half the path. not half, he says. the whole of it. the collection kept both and never smoothed it over.","name":"the rhinoceros sutta","where":"sutta nipata, chapter 1, sutta 3, verses 35 to 75. the exchange with ananda about friendship is the upaddha sutta, samyutta nikaya 45.2."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, life together · read the first chapter, then the day alone, where he argues the other side","sourceRef":"lifetogether","text":"you can't do this alone.\n[[bonhoeffer]] ran an illegal [[finkenwalde|seminary]] in germany until the [[gestapo]] shut it. then he sat down and wrote [[lifetogether|a short book]] about what living with those men had taught him.\nthe truth in your own head is weak. the same truth out of your brother's mouth is strong. you can't talk yourself out of the hole, because the one doing the talking is the one in it.\nhe called having people around you grace, nothing you can claim. his got taken."},"theme":"walk alone, walk together","id":"2026-09-30"},{"caption":"the dark one of them sits under is the thing the gods couldn't burn or budge.","date":"2026-10-01","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261001bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--marigold)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261001bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><path d='M 84 262 L 84 232 L 104 232 L 104 262 M 90 240 L 98 240' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 114 262 L 126 230 L 143 236 L 133 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 62 270 C 50 258 68 246 82 252 C 90 242 112 242 120 252 C 132 244 154 250 152 260 C 164 262 162 274 152 274 L 70 274 C 62 274 58 272 62 270 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 128 118 C 100 118 98 90 124 86 C 126 60 166 52 186 70 C 204 44 258 44 272 72 C 300 54 342 62 348 88 C 384 76 430 82 444 100 C 480 92 530 100 548 112 C 500 126 300 130 128 118 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 548 112 C 580 120 600 160 606 222'/><path d='M 600 214 L 606 224 L 613 215'/><circle cx='226' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 226 198 L 226 240 M 226 240 C 206 240 196 258 211 262 M 226 240 C 246 240 256 258 241 262 M 226 214 L 212 240 M 226 214 L 240 240'/><path d='M 222 176 L 223 174 M 231 176 L 232 174'/><path d='M 608 262 C 608 250 610 242 616 234 M 608 262 C 606 254 603 250 599 247'/><circle cx='516' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 516 194 L 516 234 M 516 234 L 504 262 M 516 234 L 529 262 M 516 208 L 552 228 M 516 208 L 500 228'/><path d='M 562 236 C 551 230 556 218 562 209 C 567 218 574 228 562 236 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><circle cx='712' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 715 190 L 724 234 M 724 234 L 712 262 M 724 234 L 740 262 M 718 206 L 740 216 M 718 206 L 700 220'/><path d='M 690 178 C 676 174 666 182 652 178 M 688 190 C 676 190 668 196 656 194 M 642 186 L 634 187'/><\/g><g transform='translate(82,205) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"the cloud of unknowing, chapters 4 to 7 · they're short, read the four in one sitting","sourceRef":"cloud","text":"you can't think your way there.\nin the 1300s an englishman nobody can name wrote [[cloud|a manual]] for a twenty four year old who wanted god. rule one: quit trying to understand him.\ngod can be loved, he says, but not thought. every idea you've got about god, even a holy one, sits between you and him.\nso shove everything you know under a [[forgetting|cloud of forgetting]]. what's left is dark. stay in the dark. don't fix it."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you sure about that you've never once checked?","refs":{"agni":{"lines":"the fire god of the vedas, india's oldest scriptures. in those old hymns he's second only to indra, the king of the gods.\nhe's every kind of fire. the sun, lightning, the cooking fire at home, the fire of the sacrifice. offerings were poured into fire, so he's called the mouth of the gods, the one who carries the gift up and runs messages between people and gods.\nin the kena story he's sent first. the spirit asks what he can do, and he says he could burn everything there is on earth. it puts a straw in front of him. he goes at it with all his might and can't burn it.\nfor today: he wasn't lying about what fire can do. he was wrong about whose it was.","name":"agni","where":"kena upanishad, part 3. the hymns to agni are all through the rig veda, the oldest of the vedas."},"cloud":{"lines":"a book about one kind of prayer, written in english in the second half of the 1300s. whoever wrote it never signed it. the best guess is a priest from the east midlands of england, and plenty of people call him a monk, but nobody knows. seventeen handwritten copies survive.\nit's a letter from a teacher to one student, and the student is young. in chapter 4 the book has him say he's twenty four and has never once paid attention to how he spent his time.\nthe teaching is two clouds. above you, between you and god, is a cloud of unknowing. he calls it a darkness, and says by darkness he only means not knowing. below you, you put a cloud of forgetting over everything else. then you stay there and keep reaching up with what he calls a sharp dart of longing love. the line the whole book hangs on is in chapter 6: he may well be loved, but not thought.\nhe isn't against thinking. he says thinking about god's kindness is good, and then tells you to tread it down anyway while you're doing this, because whatever you're thinking about is standing between you and god. he got the idea from a much older writer who went by dionysius.\nhere's why it's on this page. it was written for somebody young who wanted to get god right. the first thing it does is take getting it right off the table.","name":"the cloud of unknowing","where":"the cloud of unknowing, chapters 4 to 7. evelyn underhill's edition keeps the old wording and is easy to find. clifton wolters put it into modern english."},"forgetting":{"lines":"the book has two clouds, and this is the one people forget about. the cloud of unknowing is above you, between you and god, and you can't do anything about that one. the cloud of forgetting is the one you make. chapter 5.\nwhat goes under it is everything. every creature that ever was, is, or will be, and everything any of them ever did. not because they're bad. his reason is that whatever you're thinking about is, for that moment, above you, and between you and god.\nthen he goes further. thoughts about god's kindness, the saints, the angels, the joys of heaven. those go under too. in chapter 7 a thought shows up asking, what is this god you want? he says to tell it you have no skill in him, and then tell it to go back down, even if it looks holy and looks like it's trying to help.\nhis trick for doing it: take one little word of one syllable, god or love, and hold on to it, and beat every thought down with it.\nfor today: forgetting isn't being stupid. it's what you do with everything you're sure of while you sit there. you put it down, and you find out what's left without it.","name":"the cloud of forgetting","where":"the cloud of unknowing, chapter 5 for the cloud of forgetting, chapter 7 for the one little word."},"jesus":{"lines":"a jewish carpenter from a nothing village in the north of what's now israel. born around 4 bc. around the year 30 he spent maybe three years walking around telling stories and healing people, eating with exactly the people everybody else avoided.\nthe roman occupiers executed him as a troublemaker. his friends said they saw him alive afterward, and about a third of the world now calls itself christian after him.\nstrip away two thousand years of churches and he's the one who keeps sitting down with the person everybody else wrote off.\non this page he's only a yardstick. scholars put the kena around the middle of the thousand years before him. it was already old when he was born.","name":"jesus","where":"mark, the shortest of the four gospels, in one sitting. then matthew 5 through 7, the sermon on the mount."},"kena":{"lines":"the upanishads are the old hindu wisdom texts, written down roughly between 800 and 200 bc, that sit at the end of the vedas, india's oldest scriptures. upanishad means something like sitting down near, as in near a teacher.\nthe kena is one of the short ones. thirty four verses in four parts, two in poetry and two in plain prose. it belongs to the sama veda. kena means by whom, or by what.\nnobody has a firm date for it. scholars put it somewhere around the middle of the thousand years before jesus, and one of them files it as older than buddhism. so older than jesus is safe, by centuries. how many centuries is a guess.\nwhat it's after is brahman, the ground of everything, the thing behind your own seeing and hearing and thinking. part two says you can't hold it as a thing you know. in max müller's old translation: he by whom it is not thought, by him it is thought. he by whom it is thought, knows it not.\npart three tells the same thing as a story, and that's the right page. the gods have a victory and decide it was all them. the text says brahman won it for them.","name":"the kena upanishad","where":"kena upanishad, part 2, verse 3, for the line. part 3 for the story, and the start of part 4 for who the spirit was. max müller's translation calls it the talavakara upanishad."},"vayu":{"lines":"the wind god of the vedas, india's oldest scriptures. a messenger of the gods, and close to indra, their king. the upanishads also call him the life breath of the world.\nin the later epics he's the father of hanuman, the monkey god who leaps the ocean, and of bhima, the strongman.\nin the kena story he goes second, after fire has come back beaten. the spirit asks what he can do. he says he could pick up everything there is on earth. same straw. he goes at it with all his might and can't lift it.\nfor today: two of the biggest gods there are, one after the other, and a straw. the story doesn't say they were weak. it says the strength was on loan.","name":"vayu","where":"kena upanishad, part 3."},"yaksha":{"lines":"in indian stories a yaksha is a nature spirit. mostly kind, sometimes mischievous, now and then a lot worse. they guard treasure buried in the ground and in the roots of trees, they change shape, and people honored them as the guardian of a town or a lake or a well.\nin the kena it's just the word the gods use for the thing that shows up in front of them, because they have no idea what it is. max müller translates it sprite. some strange spirit.\nit's brahman, the ground of everything, the one that actually won their victory for them. they're looking straight at where their own power comes from and can't tell what it is. so they send fire over to find out, and then wind.\nwhen indra, the king of the gods, walks up to it himself, it's gone. a goddess named uma is standing there instead, and she tells him what it was, and whose victory it was.\nfor today: the thing the gods couldn't recognize is the thing they were sure they didn't need.","name":"the yaksha","where":"kena upanishad, part 3, and the first lines of part 4 for uma's answer."}},"right":{"lineage":"hindu","source":"kena upanishad, parts 2 and 3 · it's four short parts, read all of it","sourceRef":"kena","text":"if you think you've got it, you don't.\nthe [[kena|kena upanishad]] is a few pages long and older than [[jesus]]. it says it flat: the ones who say they understand, don't. the ones who know they don't, do.\nthen a story. the gods win a victory and strut around taking the credit. a [[yaksha|spirit]] sets a blade of grass in front of them. [[agni|fire]] can't burn it. [[vayu|wind]] can't budge it.\nthe strongest beings there are couldn't move a piece of grass. a minute earlier they'd been sure."},"theme":"the ones who are sure","id":"2026-10-01"},{"caption":"what one of them put down is lying right where the other one can reach it.","date":"2026-10-02","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261002bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261002bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><circle cx='84' cy='170' r='14'/><path d='M 76 186 L 92 186 L 90 226 L 78 226 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 81 226 L 69 262 M 87 226 L 93 262 M 76 192 L 56 210 M 92 192 L 112 210'/><path d='M 56 210 l -5 -3 M 56 210 l -6 2 M 112 210 l 5 -3 M 112 210 l 6 2'/><circle cx='176' cy='164' r='14'/><path d='M 168 180 L 184 180 L 182 220 L 170 220 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 173 220 L 161 262 M 179 220 L 185 262 M 168 186 L 148 204 M 184 186 L 204 204'/><path d='M 148 204 l -5 -3 M 148 204 l -6 2 M 204 204 l 5 -3 M 204 204 l 6 2'/><circle cx='268' cy='172' r='14'/><path d='M 260 188 L 276 188 L 274 228 L 262 228 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 265 228 L 253 262 M 271 228 L 277 262 M 260 194 L 240 212 M 276 194 L 296 212'/><path d='M 240 212 l -5 -3 M 240 212 l -6 2 M 296 212 l 5 -3 M 296 212 l 6 2'/><path d='M 346 254 L 428 254 M 346 250 L 346 254'/><path d='M 428 251 L 452 247 L 455 258 L 430 258 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 412 254 q 5 8 10 0'/><circle cx='498' cy='190' r='14'/><path d='M 508 200 L 536 228 M 536 228 L 522 262 M 536 228 L 552 262 M 514 207 L 476 240 M 514 207 L 528 236'/><path d='M 476 240 l -7 1 M 476 240 l -3 6'/><circle cx='676' cy='247' r='14'/><path d='M 690 250 L 738 254 M 738 254 L 764 247 M 738 254 L 766 260 M 702 251 L 712 262 M 702 251 L 720 238'/><\/g><g transform='translate(28,244) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"muslim","source":"eknath easwaran, nonviolent soldier of islam · a short warm life of him in english, read all of it","sourceRef":"ghaffarkhan","text":"put it down.\n[[ghaffarkhan|abdul ghaffar khan]] came from the [[pashtuns]], people the british wrote off as born killers. he raised an army out of them, [[khudaikhidmatgar|a hundred thousand red shirts]], sworn to no weapons and no revenge.\nhe said he was giving them a weapon no police or army could stand against. the [[muhammad|prophet]]'s weapon. patience and being in the right.\nin 1930 british troops opened fire on them in [[qissakhwani|a peshawar bazaar]]. they kept walking up, unarmed, row after row.\ntoday is [[gandhi]]'s birthday. khan did it with men raised on the blood feud."},"mode":"disagree","question":"who would you have to watch get hurt before you'd pick it up?","refs":{"abwehr":{"lines":"germany's military intelligence service, the armed forces' spies. it ran from 1920 to 1944, and from 1935 an admiral named wilhelm canaris was in charge of it.\nit was also where a handful of the men who wanted hitler gone were hiding in plain sight. a handful is the word. by one count, out of a staff of some thirteen thousand, maybe fifty were really against the nazis. one was hans oster, who ran its central office. another was hans von dohnanyi, bonhoeffer's brother-in-law, who was in on the planning.\nin october 1940 dohnanyi got bonhoeffer an assignment there. on paper, a pastor with church friends all over europe was useful to germany. in fact it kept him out of the army and gave him a reason to travel. between 1941 and 1942 he made several trips out of the country and told church contacts in geneva and at the vatican what the resistance was planning. in may 1942 he met an english bishop, george bell, who passed word to the british government.\nhe and dohnanyi were arrested on april 5, 1943. hitler shut the abwehr down in february 1944 and the ss took over its work. canaris and oster were executed too, just before the war ended.\nfor today, that's the exact size of what he did. a courier. he knew of the plans to kill hitler and approved of them, and he never touched a bomb.","name":"the abwehr","where":"bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, the chapters on the war years."},"bonhoeffer":{"lines":"a german pastor and theologian, born in 1906. he saw what hitler was the minute he took over in 1933 and said so, while most of the churches in germany went along. his book on what it costs to follow jesus, the cost of discipleship, came out in 1937.\nfriends got him to new york in june 1939 to keep him safe. he lasted a few weeks and sailed home. then he joined the resistance, a circle inside german military intelligence who tried more than once to kill hitler. he carried messages abroad for them. he knew of the plans to kill hitler and approved of them, but he took no part in the attempts himself.\nhe was arrested in april 1943, at first for helping jews get out of germany. he was hanged at flossenbürg, a nazi concentration camp, on april 9, 1945, two weeks before the americans got there and about a month before germany surrendered. he was thirty nine.\nhere's the trouble this page is about. he'd been a pacifist, someone who won't use violence at all. back in 1933 he had written that the church's job was not just to bandage the victims under the wheel but to put a spoke in the wheel itself. when it came to it, he helped men who meant to kill. he never called it innocent. he accepted the guilt and did it anyway.","name":"dietrich bonhoeffer","where":"eberhard bethge's dietrich bonhoeffer: a biography, written by his closest friend, for what he did. ethics and letters and papers from prison for what he thought about it."},"ethicsbook":{"lines":"the book bonhoeffer meant to be his big one. he worked on it during the years he was in the resistance, and it was still in pieces when he was arrested in april 1943. his friend eberhard bethge put the pieces together and published them in 1949, four years after he was hanged.\nthe part today's page uses is called the structure of responsible life. he drafted it in the summer of 1942, while he was carrying messages.\nthe line is this, in the newer english translation: everyone who acts responsibly becomes guilty. an older translation has the rest of it. when a man takes guilt upon himself in responsibility, he puts it on himself and no one else. before other men he is justified by dire necessity. before himself he is acquitted by his conscience. but before god he hopes only for grace.\nhe doesn't make it about his own year. he says it about everybody who ever acts for somebody else. his reason is jesus, who took on guilt that wasn't his. so a conscience that's been freed, he says, is free to bear guilt for the sake of the neighbor.\nfor today: he's not saying killing is fine if the cause is good. he's saying it isn't fine, and doing it anyway, and answering for it himself. before god, he says, all he hopes for is grace.","name":"ethics","where":"dietrich bonhoeffer, ethics, the section called the structure of responsible life, inside the chapter history and good. the newer translation is volume 6 of the dietrich bonhoeffer works in english."},"gandhi":{"lines":"an indian lawyer, born october 2, 1869, who became the leader of india's fight to get out from under british rule. he died in delhi on january 30, 1948.\nhe figured out how to fight an empire without firing a shot, by getting millions of ordinary people to break unfair laws on purpose and take the beating without hitting back. every protest you've seen where people sit down in the road and let themselves get dragged off runs on his method.\nhe read a hindu scripture and the sermon on the mount side by side. the thing to take from him is that you can refuse to hate someone and still refuse to obey them.\nhe's on both of today's pages. ghaffar khan was his close friend, close enough to get called the frontier gandhi, and the two toured the frontier together in 1938. bonhoeffer, over in europe, had a chance in the 1930s to go to india and study nonviolent resistance with him. he talked about it, made plans, and never went. he went back to germany.\nso today's two pages are two people who took him seriously and came out in different places.","name":"mohandas gandhi","where":"his autobiography, the story of my experiments with truth. then the sermon on the mount, matthew chapters 5 through 7, which he said went straight to his heart."},"ghaffarkhan":{"lines":"a landowner's son from the pashtuns, the hill people on both sides of the border between what's now pakistan and afghanistan, where an insult could be paid back in blood generations later. born in 1890, died in 1988.\nin 1929 he started an army that swore off violence, men in red shirts, somewhere over a hundred thousand of them by the usual estimates. he said he got the idea from the prophet muhammad. he told them he was going to give them a weapon the police and the army would not be able to stand against. it is the weapon of the prophet, he said, but you are not aware of it. that weapon is patience and righteousness. every account gives those same words. none gives the day or the place he said them.\nhe was in gandhi's inner circle, which is how he got called the frontier gandhi. he spent something like a third of his long life locked up, first by the british and then by pakistan. somewhere between twenty seven and thirty years, depending on who's counting.\nthe movement didn't last. pakistan banned the red shirts in 1948 and broke them. he kept saying the same thing into his nineties.\nhere's why he's on this page, on gandhi's birthday. everybody knows the one. hardly anybody knows the muslim next door who did the same thing, with the people everyone said couldn't.","name":"abdul ghaffar khan","where":"eknath easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam, first published in 1984 as a man to match his mountains. then my life and struggle, his own memoir, in english since 1969."},"khudaikhidmatgar":{"lines":"pashto for the servants of god, the nonviolent movement abdul ghaffar khan founded on the north-west frontier in 1929, though sources put the start anywhere from late 1929 to 1930. members swore in god's name to serve without self interest, to take no revenge, to stay nonviolent and to give time every day to work for others.\ntheir shirts were dyed brick red at a tannery because white ones got dirty, so everyone called them the red shirts. estimates of its size run from about a hundred thousand to three hundred thousand.\nit joined the congress party in 1931, and the british hit it hard, with beatings and worse. after 1947 pakistan banned it in september 1948 and made mass arrests. the ban was lifted in 1972, but by then the movement had been broken.\nfor today: it was set up like an army, uniforms and all, and it had no weapons. the oath wasn't to be peaceful when that was easy. it was no revenge, sworn in a place where revenge was the rule.","name":"the khudai khidmatgar","where":"easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam tells how it started. the oath is quoted there and in most accounts of the movement."},"muhammad":{"lines":"the man islam starts with. a merchant from mecca, in arabia, who at about forty said an angel had given him words from god, and spent the next twenty two years reciting them, getting run out of town for them, and then leading a community that took over most of arabia. he lived from around 570 to 632.\nabout two billion muslims hold that he was the last prophet, and that the quran is god's speech, not his. most muslims don't picture him, so the drawing doesn't either.\nghaffar khan said there was nothing surprising in a muslim or a pathan like him taking up nonviolence. it was followed fourteen hundred years ago, he said, by the prophet all the time he was in mecca.\nthat's the early years he's pointing at, before muhammad was driven out of his hometown. the later years had battles in them. ghaffar khan built his army on the first part, and called patience the prophet's own weapon.","name":"muhammad","where":"the short chapters at the back of the quran, starting with chapter 93, the morning hours. ghaffar khan's line about mecca is quoted in easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam."},"nazi":{"lines":"hitler's party, which ran germany from 1933 to 1945. they built a police state, started the second world war, and murdered six million jews along with millions of others in the holocaust.\nthe word nazi gets thrown around a lot now. on these pages it means the actual thing, a government that jailed and killed people like bonhoeffer while most of the churches went along.\nfor today: this is what he was looking at when he decided. he wasn't weighing nonviolence against an insult or a bad law. he was weighing it against this.","name":"nazi","where":"bethge's biography of bonhoeffer tells it from inside one family. for the whole of it, any history of the holocaust."},"pashtuns":{"lines":"a people who live mostly in the south and east of afghanistan and the north-west of pakistan, somewhere around sixty to seventy million of them. they speak pashto, and they're often called the world's largest tribal society, split into hundreds of tribes. in india the usual word for them was pathan, and the british used it too.\nalongside islam, what holds them together is pashtunwali, their code of honor, older than their islam. it tells you to take in any guest, whoever he is, and ask nothing back. it also has badal, justice or revenge. badal has no time limit, and even a taunt can count as a mortal insult, which is how feuds ran for generations.\noutsiders made that the whole story. the british and the people who wrote for them had the pathan down as a warrior, hotheaded and unpredictable, violent by nature. one scholar's book on the red shirts, the pathan unarmed, is about that picture and how badly it missed.\nfor today: the red shirts' oath had them swear off badal by name. ghaffar khan didn't go looking for gentle people. he went to the ones whose own code said hit back, and asked them for the opposite.","name":"the pashtuns","where":"easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam for the world he grew up in. mukulika banerjee's the pathan unarmed for how it happened."},"qissakhwani":{"lines":"on april 23, 1930, after abdul ghaffar khan was arrested, a crowd gathered in the qissa khwani bazaar, the storytellers' market in the old city of peshawar. british armored cars drove into the square at speed and killed several people, and then troops opened fire on the unarmed crowd.\nthe account most people quote comes by way of the writer gene sharp. when those in front fell down wounded by the shots, those behind came forward with their chests bared. it says this went on from eleven in the morning till five in the evening. the same accounts say stones were thrown and an army dispatch rider was killed.\nthe official british count was twenty dead. indian and pakistani sources say up to four hundred, and ghaffar khan put it at two hundred to two hundred and fifty. the numbers are still argued over.\ntwo platoons of the garhwal rifles, indian soldiers in the british army, refused to be sent into the city against that crowd. their leaders got years in prison for it.\nfor today: this is what the oath cost. the movement was only months old. nobody in that square had been at it long enough to be good at it.","name":"the qissa khwani massacre","where":"easwaran's nonviolent soldier of islam. gene sharp's account of the rows coming forward is quoted in most histories of the day."},"sermononthemount":{"lines":"the three chapters in the gospel of matthew, chapters 5 through 7, where jesus sits on a hillside and lays out how to actually live. love your enemies. turn the other cheek. don't worry about tomorrow. don't make a show of praying.\nit's the most read, most argued-over speech in the bible. tolstoy decided it was the whole of christianity. gandhi said it went straight to his heart. bonhoeffer wrote a book asking why christians who claim it don't do it.\nif you read one thing in the bible, a lot of people on these pages would tell you to read this. it takes about fifteen minutes.\nfor today: that book of his came out in 1937. three years later he was working for men who were planning a killing. he didn't say the sermon had been wrong. he said he was guilty.","name":"the sermon on the mount","where":"matthew 5 through 7. love your enemies is near the end of chapter 5. bonhoeffer's book on it is the cost of discipleship."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"dietrich bonhoeffer, ethics, the part on the structure of responsible life · then eberhard bethge's biography for what he actually did","sourceRef":"ethicsbook","text":"sometimes you help kill him.\n[[bonhoeffer]] preached the [[sermononthemount|sermon on the mount]] like he meant it. love your enemies. he'd planned a trip to india to learn nonviolence from [[gandhi]].\nthen he saw what the [[nazi|nazis]] were doing, took a cover job in [[abwehr|german military intelligence]], and carried messages for the men planning to blow hitler up. he knew. he was for it.\nhe never called it clean. in [[ethicsbook|the book he was writing]] when they arrested him, he says whoever acts responsibly becomes guilty. you take the guilt on yourself and nobody else. before god, all you've got left to hope for is grace.\nthey hanged him."},"theme":"never hit back","id":"2026-10-02"},{"caption":"the pile one of them keeps building is the trail the other one leaves behind.","date":"2026-10-03","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261003bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261003bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><path d='M 38 118 L 116 118 M 50 118 L 50 130 M 104 118 L 104 130'/><path d='M 56 262 L 56 214 L 148 214 L 148 262' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 74 230 L 130 230 L 130 252 L 74 252 Z'/><ellipse cx='100' cy='208' rx='27' ry='6' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 127 207 L 160 202'/><circle cx='100' cy='207' r='3.5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 90 194 C 86 186 94 182 90 174 M 108 194 C 104 186 112 182 108 174'/><circle cx='196' cy='166' r='14'/><path d='M 183 171 C 176 143 216 143 209 171 L 212 186 M 183 171 L 180 186'/><path d='M 196 180 L 196 228 M 196 228 L 185 262 M 196 228 L 208 262 M 196 197 L 160 202 M 196 197 L 224 192'/><path d='M 222 180 L 234 186 L 246 180 L 246 195 L 234 201 L 222 195 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 234 186 L 234 201'/><path d='M 300 250 L 346 250 L 346 262 L 300 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 304 238 L 350 238 L 350 250 L 304 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 297 226 L 343 226 L 343 238 L 297 238 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 302 214 L 348 214 L 348 226 L 302 226 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 296 202 L 342 202 L 342 214 L 296 214 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 303 190 L 349 190 L 349 202 L 303 202 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 300 178 L 346 178 L 346 190 L 300 190 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 298 166 L 344 166 L 344 178 L 298 178 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 304 154 L 350 154 L 350 166 L 304 166 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 299 142 L 345 142 L 345 154 L 299 154 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 344 118 L 388 132 L 385 143 L 341 129 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 396 146 C 420 162 432 200 436 240' stroke-dasharray='1 12'/><path d='M 418 252 L 456 252 L 456 262 L 418 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 486 253 L 518 253 L 518 262 L 486 262 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 546 254 L 572 254 L 572 262 L 546 262 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 598 255 L 618 255 L 618 262 L 598 262 Z' fill='var(--lavender)'/><path d='M 642 257 L 655 257 L 655 262 L 642 262 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='716' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 716 198 L 716 240 M 716 240 C 696 240 686 258 701 262 M 716 240 C 736 240 746 258 731 262 M 716 214 L 704 238 L 716 236 M 716 214 L 728 238 L 716 236'/><\/g><g transform='translate(66,91) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"sor juana inés de la cruz, the reply to sor filotea · it's a letter, it's short, and she tells her whole life in it","sourceRef":"replyfilotea","text":"read everything.\n[[sorjuana|sor juana]] could read by three, in mexico, in the 1600s, when no girl got near a university. she became a nun because a convent was the one place a woman could keep a library.\n[[bishoppuebla|a bishop]] told her in print to knock it off and think about god.\n[[replyfilotea|her answer]] is one of the great fuck-yous in church history, polite the whole way through. she can't stop, she says. banned from books for three months, she studied everything else. even how an egg fries. if [[aristotle]] had cooked, he'd have written more.\ngod gave her the mind. using it was the prayer."},"mode":"disagree","question":"what do you keep studying so you don't have to do it?","refs":{"aristotle":{"lines":"a greek philosopher, 384 to 322 bc, a student of plato and the tutor of alexander the great. he started his own school in athens and wrote about nearly everything, from nature and logic to politics and the arts.\nmedieval muslim scholars called him the first teacher, and christians in europe just called him the philosopher. in sor juana's world he was the top of the shelf.\nso the line is a joke with teeth in it. she's just listed what she learned standing at a stove, and then says the biggest name in the library would have known more if he'd ever stood at one.\nfor today: it's her whole case in one sentence. the kitchen they sent her to was a place to study too. you can take her books. you can't make the world stop being interesting.","name":"aristotle","where":"the line is in the reply to sor filotea, right after the egg."},"bishoppuebla":{"lines":"manuel fernández de santa cruz, a spaniard born in 1637 who was bishop of puebla, a city in new spain, from 1676 until he died in 1699.\nin 1690 sor juana wrote out her objections to a famous sermon by a portuguese priest. the bishop printed it without asking her and put a letter of his own in front, dated november 25, 1690, and signed with a nun's name, sor filotea de la cruz. sor means sister.\nthe letter praises her mind and then says it's a pity that so great a mind stoops to the low news of the earth. he didn't want her to give up books, he wrote, only to better them by sometimes reading the book of jesus christ. that's the knock it off on today's page. it came with compliments.\nscholars split on him. most, the mexican poet octavio paz included, count him her friend. the 1990 film about her makes him one of the men who betrayed her.\nfor today: he's the reason the letter exists. he told the smartest person in the colony to aim lower, under a fake name, in print, and she answered in her own.","name":"the bishop of puebla","where":"his letter is printed in front of the reply in most editions, including alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology."},"replyfilotea":{"lines":"sor juana's answer to the bishop of puebla, dated march 1, 1691, from her convent in mexico city. it's addressed to sor filotea, the nun's name he had signed.\nit's part life story and part argument. she tells how she learned to read before she was three, lists learned women from the bible to her own time, and reads paul's order that women keep silent in church as a rule about pulpits, not about study or writing. it's been called the first feminist manifesto.\nthe part on today's page goes like this. a superior at the convent, very holy and very simple, decided study was a matter for the inquisition and ordered her not to read. she obeyed for the three months or so the woman was in charge, and didn't pick up a book. but not studying at all, she says, is not in her power. so she studied everything god had made. how the walls of a long room seem to lean toward each other. a top some girls were spinning, and why it kept going.\nthen she adds the kitchen. an egg holds together and fries in butter or oil, and falls to shreds in syrup. and the line, in margaret sayers peden's translation: had aristotle prepared victuals, he would have written more.\nshe's careful the whole way through. she says she wants no trouble with the inquisition and calls herself ignorant. reading it, you can't always tell where the humility stops and the irony starts. that's the polite part.","name":"the reply to sor filotea","where":"the reply to sor filotea. the ban and the kitchen come a little past the middle. margaret sayers peden's translation, a woman of genius, or alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology."},"sorjuana":{"lines":"a poet and nun in mexico city in the late 1600s, when mexico was a spanish colony called new spain. born in 1648 or 1651, nobody's sure. she taught herself nearly everything, because girls weren't let into the university.\nalmost all of what's known about her childhood is her own telling. before she was three, she says, she tagged along behind an older sister to a woman who taught reading, told the teacher her mother wanted her taught too, and could read before her mother found out. at six or seven she heard there was a university in mexico city and begged her mother to change her clothes and send her. translators give it as dress me as a boy. her mother said no, so she read her grandfather's books.\nshe took her vows in 1669 and was blunt about why. she had no wish at all to marry, and the convent was the least unsuitable choice open to her. what she'd wanted was to live alone, with nothing to get in the way of study. she got a cell of her own, instruments, visitors, and a library. the number that gets repeated is four thousand books. nobody has a catalog.\nthe hard parts. she owned a person, an enslaved woman named juana de san josé, and in 1684 sold her to her own sister. and about two years after her great letter she went quiet. her books went too, sold for the poor in most tellings, taken in some. she died in april 1695 nursing her sisters through a plague.\nfor today: the right page would tell her to put the books down. in the end they did go. whether that was surrender or peace, the record doesn't say.","name":"sor juana inés de la cruz","where":"the reply to sor filotea, where she tells her own life. margaret sayers peden's translation is called a woman of genius, and alan trueblood's a sor juana anthology has the reply and the poems together."},"tao":{"lines":"chinese for the way, or the road. in lao tzu's book it means the way everything works underneath everything, the flow the whole world moves in, which you can follow but can't name or grab.\ntaoism, the chinese tradition that grew up around the idea, says the best life is lived going with it, like a boat going with the current. the first line of the tao te ching says the tao you can talk about isn't the real one.\nfor today: that's why more learning doesn't get you closer to it. learning is names and grabbing. the book says the way is what's left when you put those down.","name":"the tao","where":"tao te ching, chapter 1 for the way that can't be named. chapter 48 for following it by dropping things."},"taoteching":{"lines":"the short chinese book credited to lao tzu, eighty one tiny chapters, about five thousand characters in all. the title means something like the book of the way and its power. it probably took shape over a couple of centuries before about 250 bc. so it's not about her. it's close to two thousand years older than she is.\nit's about water, emptiness, not forcing, and how the soft outlasts the hard. it's also, oddly, advice to rulers, telling them the less they meddle the better things go.\nchapter 48 is six lines. in gia-fu feng and jane english's translation: in the pursuit of learning, every day something is acquired. in the pursuit of tao, every day something is dropped. less and less is done until non-action is achieved. when nothing is done, nothing is left undone. the last two lines turn to the ruler. the world is ruled by letting things take their course.\nthe other line is, in the same translation, give up learning, and put an end to your troubles. in most translations it's the first line of chapter 20. james legge has it there too: when we renounce learning we have no troubles. d.c. lau prints it with chapter 19 instead. chapter 20 runs about thirty short lines and goes somewhere else after that.\nfor today: it isn't saying be stupid. it's saying the piling up can turn into the problem, and that there's a kind of getting somewhere that only happens by setting things down.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapter 48, and the first line of chapter 20, or chapter 19 in d.c. lau's translation. gia-fu feng and jane english's version is the plainest."},"wuwei":{"lines":"chinese for doing without doing, or effortless action. it's the heart of the tao te ching. translators of chapter 48 usually give it as non-action.\nit's not laziness. it's acting without forcing, the way a good carpenter follows the grain of the wood or a swimmer floats instead of thrashing. you do things, but you stop fighting the way things want to go.\nathletes call it being in the zone. musicians call it the song playing itself.\nfor today: it's where all the dropping is headed. the chapter doesn't end with a person who does nothing. it ends with nothing left undone.","name":"wu wei","where":"tao te ching, chapter 48. less and less is done until non-action is achieved."}},"right":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 48 · then chapter 20. any translation, they're both short. a few print that line in 19","sourceRef":"taoteching","text":"drop one thing a day.\nthe [[taoteching|tao te ching]] has a chapter a few lines long, aimed right at people like her.\nchase learning and every day you pick something up. follow [[tao|the way]] and every day you put something down. less, then less again, until you're [[wuwei|not forcing anything]]. then nothing is left undone.\nanother chapter says it flat. give up learning and your troubles are over.\nthe stack of books by your bed is one more way to not sit still."},"theme":"learn it or unlearn it","id":"2026-10-03"},{"caption":"the wide circle one of them used to keep is the mouth the other one walks into.","date":"2026-10-04","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261004bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261004bg)' stroke='none'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><ellipse cx='98' cy='216' rx='32' ry='13' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 76 226 L 72 262 M 88 228 L 90 262 M 108 228 L 106 262 M 120 226 L 124 262'/><path d='M 124 208 L 142 182'/><path d='M 142 182 L 160 188 L 157 197 L 140 193 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 144 182 l 1 -9 M 68 212 C 58 216 56 230 60 240'/><path d='M 176 258 C 186 96 388 96 400 258' stroke-dasharray='1 13'/><circle cx='262' cy='186' r='14'/><path d='M 258 199 L 250 236 M 250 236 L 238 262 M 250 236 L 262 262 M 256 210 L 292 206 M 256 214 L 290 222'/><circle cx='306' cy='188' r='14'/><path d='M 294 182 L 318 186 M 294 190 L 316 194' opacity='.6'/><path d='M 308 202 L 312 238 M 312 238 L 302 262 M 312 238 L 324 262 M 310 214 L 292 214 M 310 214 L 330 230'/><circle cx='291' cy='214' r='4' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 326 232 L 340 232 L 337 222 L 329 222 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><path d='M 333 232 L 333 237'/><path d='M 400 258 C 412 60 744 60 756 262' fill='var(--paper)' fill-opacity='.35'/><path d='M 0 262 C 120 258 260 266 400 262 S 680 258 800 262'/><circle cx='606' cy='148' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='664' cy='148' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='602' cy='152' r='3'/><circle cx='660' cy='152' r='3'/><path d='M 582 126 L 622 134 M 688 126 L 648 134'/><path d='M 548 218 C 580 186 690 186 722 218 C 700 262 570 262 548 218 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 566 208 l 9 13 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -14 l 9 14 l 9 -13'/><circle cx='492' cy='192' r='14'/><path d='M 494 206 L 498 238 M 498 238 L 486 262 M 498 238 L 512 262 M 496 216 L 522 214 M 496 216 L 480 232'/><\/g><g transform='translate(84,176) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"francis of assisi, the testament · it's a few pages. then thomas of celano's first life for the two miles, and the legend of the three companions for the horse and the coin","sourceRef":"testament","text":"kiss the thing that makes you gag.\n[[francis]] was a rich kid who couldn't stand [[leper|lepers]]. he'd hold his nose two miles from their houses.\ndying, he dictated [[testament|a short last statement]], and it starts with them. the sight of them used to be bitter, he says. then god led him in among them, and he cared for them. and the bitter turned sweet, in his soul and his body.\nthe early biographies add the scene: off the horse, a coin in the man's hand, a kiss.\nhe didn't get over the disgust and then go. he went. the going changed it."},"mode":"agree","question":"what are you keeping two miles away from?","refs":{"demon":{"lines":"this is a story, not a record. it's the first one in the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, a book put together in 1488, more than three hundred years after he lived.\nin the book there are five of them, with eyes as large as saucers. one is sitting on his bed preaching, two are listening, one is cooking, and the last is studying his books. he tries a polite song first, in case they're the local gods. then a fierce meditation and a spell. then he preaches to them, and means it. they stay where they are and stare.\nthen he remembers that all beings and everything he sees are of his own mind, and he stops trying to throw them out. he sings them a welcome. i pray you, stay. we will discourse and play together. then he gets up and goes straight at them, and they shrink back, swirl together like a whirlpool, merge into one, and vanish. that's how the book ends it. there's no mouth.\nthe mouth is in another telling. jack kornfield quotes it, and the american nun pema chödrön tells it her own way. there it's seven demons. three go when he praises them, three more vanish like a rainbow when he sings, and the last is the vicious one. he places himself in its mouth. it can't eat him, and it vanishes. in pema chödrön's words he says, just eat me up if you want to. today's page takes the cave from the book and the ending from that telling.\nfor today: every version agrees on what doesn't work. arguing with it, teaching it, and ordering it out. what works is letting it stay, and then going closer than you want to.","name":"the demons in the cave","where":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, the first story, the tale of red rock jewel valley. the telling with the mouth is quoted in jack kornfield's after the ecstasy, the laundry."},"francis":{"lines":"a rich cloth merchant's kid from assisi, a hill town in the middle of italy, born in 1181 or 1182. he spent his youth on parties, songs and good clothes, and he wanted to be a knight. he went to war against the town next door, got captured, and spent about a year in prison. he came home sick and not quite the same.\na few years later, riding outside assisi, he met a leper, and instead of riding past he got down and kissed him. after that the rest went fast. he gave back his father's money in front of the bishop, took off all his clothes and handed those back too, and walked out owning nothing.\nbrothers gathered around him, begging and preaching, and became the franciscans. the stories have him preaching to birds, making a deal with a wolf, and turning out to be the happiest person anyone had ever met.\nhe died on the evening of october 3, 1226, laid on the bare ground at his own asking. his feast day is october 4. that's today.\nhere's why the leper is the page. when he was dying and looked back at how it started, he didn't start with the war or the prison or the voice in the church. he started with the people he couldn't stand to look at.","name":"francis of assisi","where":"his own testament, which is a few pages. the little flowers of st francis for the legends. thomas of celano's first life, written within a few years of his death."},"leper":{"lines":"leprosy is a bacterial disease that eats away at nerves and skin, so people lose feeling and then fingers, toes, and faces. in the middle ages it was the most feared disease there was, and people thought it was a punishment for sin.\nlepers had to live outside the town, carry a clapper or bell so people could get out of the way, and nobody touched them.\nit's curable now with antibiotics. calling someone a leper still means the person nobody will go near.\nfor today: francis's disgust wasn't unusual. it was what everybody felt, and he had more of it than most. his first biographer says that in his wild years he'd hold his nostrils at the sight of their houses from two miles off. that's the man who got down off the horse.","name":"leprosy","where":"thomas of celano's first life of francis, the chapter on the lepers, for the two miles. francis's testament for his own three sentences about it."},"milarepa":{"lines":"a tibetan of the eleventh century, and tibet's favorite saint. nearly everything told about him comes from a life story and a book of songs written down about three hundred and fifty years after he died, so very little is known about the real man.\nin the story, an uncle robbed his family blind after his father died, and his mother sent him off to learn black magic. he used it to kill thirty five people. then the guilt ate him alive. he spent years hauling rocks for a brutal teacher named marpa, and then decades alone in mountain caves, half starved, living on nettles, singing what he'd learned.\nhunters and villagers came across him and he sang to them, and some of them stayed. the songs are still sung.\nfor today: he's in a cave by himself because of what he did. so when the story fills the cave with demons and he remembers they're his own mind, that isn't a figure of speech for him. he knows exactly what's in there.","name":"milarepa","where":"the life of milarepa, in lobsang lhalungpa's or andrew quintman's translation. then the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, where the cave stories are."},"testament":{"lines":"the last thing francis left his brothers. he spent his final days dictating it, and died on the evening of october 3, 1226.\nit opens with the lepers. in the standard english translation: when i was in sin, it seemed too bitter for me to see lepers. and the lord himself led me among them and i showed mercy to them. and when i left them, what had seemed bitter to me was turned into sweetness of soul and body. then he says he waited a little and left the world. that's his whole account of how he changed. three sentences.\nnotice what isn't in it. no horse, no coin, no kiss. those come from the lives written after he died. thomas of celano's first one, from 1228, has the two miles and the held nose, and says that one day he met a leper and kissed him. the legend of the three companions, begun in the 1240s, has him riding near assisi, getting down, giving the man a coin and kissing his hand.\nthe rest of the testament is a dying man telling his brothers not to let it go soft. nobody showed him what to do, he says. god did. work with your hands. don't go asking rome for favors. don't add explanations to the rule, or to these words.\nfor today: he says god led him in and the bitter turned sweet when he left. he doesn't say he felt ready first. the order on the page is his order.","name":"the testament of francis","where":"the testament, the first four sentences. it's in any collection of francis's own writings. the collection called francis of assisi: early documents has it alongside the early lives."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"the hundred thousand songs of milarepa, the first story, the tale of red rock jewel valley · the mouth is from another telling, quoted in jack kornfield's after the ecstasy, the laundry","sourceRef":"demon","text":"walk into its mouth.\n[[milarepa]] comes home to his cave in the tibetan mountains and it's full of [[demon|demons]]. eyes like saucers. one's on his bed. one's reading his books.\nhe preaches at them. nothing. then he remembers that whatever he sees is his own mind, and says fine. stay. let's talk.\nthey all leave but one. the worst one. he walks over and puts himself in its mouth. go ahead, eat me.\nthen that one's gone too.\nyou know exactly which one is still sitting in your cave."},"theme":"walk toward it","id":"2026-10-04"},{"caption":"follow one man's babble far enough and it turns into the other man's silence.","date":"2026-10-05","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261005bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--teal)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261005bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 80 252 160 260 260 256 S 420 254 520 256 S 700 258 800 256'/><path d='M 0 204 C 36 170 110 168 158 206'/><circle cx='72' cy='138' r='8'/><path d='M 72 146 L 72 166 M 72 166 L 67 180 M 72 166 L 78 180 M 72 152 L 83 156 M 84 134 L 84 181'/><ellipse cx='60' cy='238' rx='20' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 47 247 L 46 256 M 55 249 L 55 256 M 66 249 L 66 256 M 73 247 L 74 256 M 40 234 L 35 230'/><circle cx='85' cy='224' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 82 218 q -5 -8 -11 -7 M 87 217 q -2 -9 -8 -11 M 88 231 l 1 6'/><ellipse cx='160' cy='238' rx='20' ry='11' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 147 247 L 146 256 M 155 249 L 155 256 M 166 249 L 166 256 M 173 247 L 174 256 M 140 234 L 135 230'/><circle cx='185' cy='226' r='7' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 182 220 q -5 -8 -11 -7 M 187 219 q -2 -9 -8 -11 M 188 233 l 1 6'/><circle cx='270' cy='172' r='14'/><ellipse cx='278' cy='177' rx='3' ry='4'/><path d='M 268 186 L 262 224 M 262 224 L 288 252 L 250 254 M 267 198 L 244 172 M 267 198 L 298 200'/><ellipse cx='318' cy='250' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><ellipse cx='342' cy='250' rx='10' ry='4' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 313 247 L 320 253 M 337 247 L 344 253'/><path d='M 362 243 L 390 243 M 364 243 L 364 253 M 371 243 L 371 253 M 378 243 L 378 253 M 385 243 L 385 253'/><path d='M 286 170 C 300 150 318 138 326 150 C 334 164 308 168 314 146 C 320 118 350 112 356 128 C 362 146 334 148 342 124 C 350 96 384 92 388 110 C 392 128 366 126 374 106 C 382 84 410 84 424 96 C 438 108 450 84 468 92 C 484 99 496 88 512 92 C 526 95 536 91 548 92 L 580 92'/><path d='M 596 92 L 642 92' stroke-dasharray='11 13'/><path d='M 658 92 L 712 92' stroke-dasharray='1 16'/><circle cx='612' cy='172' r='14'/><path d='M 616 178 L 623 178'/><path d='M 612 186 L 612 228 M 612 228 L 638 228 L 640 256 M 612 228 L 630 233 L 630 256'/><path d='M 612 198 L 633 200 L 627 181 M 612 202 L 640 214 L 666 213'/><path d='M 597 233 L 624 233 M 601 233 L 599 256 M 620 233 L 621 256'/><path d='M 656 218 L 742 218 M 664 218 L 664 256 M 734 218 L 734 256'/><path d='M 678 215 Q 690 205 702 215 Q 714 205 726 215 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 752 256 L 752 246 L 790 246 L 790 256 M 757 246 L 757 237 L 786 237 L 786 246'/><\/g><g transform='translate(759,210) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"muslim","source":"rumi, the masnavi, book 2, the story of moses and the shepherd · nicholson's translation is the literal one","sourceRef":"masnavi","text":"say it wrong. he doesn't care.\n[[rumi]] tells it in the [[masnavi]]. [[moses]] hears a shepherd praying. god, where are you, so i can fix your sandals and comb your hair. i'll kill your lice. i'll kiss your little hand and rub your little foot and sweep your room at bedtime.\nmoses loses it. lice? feet? stuff cotton in your mouth. the shepherd tears his shirt and runs for the desert.\nthen god comes for moses. you drove my servant away from me. did i send you to bring people close or to split them off? i don't look at the words. i look at what's burning inside.\nthe one with the correct theology got yelled at."},"mode":"disagree","question":"when you talk to god, or whatever you call it, who are you picturing?","refs":{"guideperplexed":{"lines":"maimonides' book of philosophy, finished around 1190 and written in arabic in hebrew letters. it's set up as a long letter to a former student, joseph ben judah, who couldn't see how the torah, the first five books of the hebrew bible, and the science of his day could both be true.\nits most famous move is about god. the bible's pictures of god aren't meant literally. god has no body. and the most you can truly say about god is what god is not. not dead. not weak. not ignorant. every no, he says, gets you a little nearer.\nchapters 58 and 59 of the first part are where he lands it. we can know that god is. we can't know what god is. so a longer list of praises is a worse one, and the best praise is to stop talking. he backs it with the story from the talmud and the line from the psalms on this page.\neven great, mighty and awesome, he says, we only get to say because moses said them first and the old sages put them in the prayers. nobody gets to add to the list.\nchristians read it too. thomas aquinas cites him as rabbi moses. and the shepherd in rumi's poem is exactly who it was written against.","name":"the guide for the perplexed","where":"the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 58 and 59. m. friedländer's english translation is the old public one."},"hanina":{"lines":"a rabbi is a jewish teacher and judge of the law. this one is in the talmud, the huge record of the early rabbis arguing, which just calls him rabbi hanina. that's most likely hanina bar hama, who lived in the town of sepphoris, in the land of israel, and died around the year 250. he worked as a doctor too, same as maimonides.\nthe story is a few lines long. a man gets up to lead the prayers in front of him and starts in on god. great, mighty, awesome. those three are in the prayer book. then he keeps going. powerful, strong, fearless, steadfast, honored.\nin the talmud hanina doesn't cut him off. he waits until the man has run all the way out, and then he asks: have you finished all the praises of your master? even the first three, he says, we'd never say on our own. moses said them, and the sages put them in the prayers. and you go and add all that?\nthen the king. he has gold coins by the thousands, millions in maimonides' telling, and people praise him for his silver. isn't that an insult?\nmaimonides picked the story up more than nine hundred years later and built his case on it. the man wasn't saying anything false. he was saying too much, and every extra word made god smaller.","name":"rabbi hanina","where":"the babylonian talmud, berakhot 33b. that's the volume on blessings, page 33, second side. maimonides retells it in the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapter 59."},"maimonides":{"lines":"moses ben maimon, a rabbi and a doctor. a rabbi is a jewish teacher and judge of the law. born in cordoba, in muslim spain, in 1138, though older books say 1135. died in egypt in 1204. jews call him the rambam.\nhis family was driven out of spain when he was a boy, and they wandered for years before settling in egypt, next to cairo. his younger brother traded in gems and paid the bills, until his ship went down with the family's money on it. maimonides stayed in bed for about a year. then he got up and went to work as a doctor, and ended up a physician at the sultan's court.\nhe wrote the mishneh torah, a code of all of jewish law with the arguments taken out, and the guide for the perplexed, the hard book this page comes from. he lived his whole life among muslims and wrote his philosophy in arabic.\nhe also wrote a list of what a jew has to believe, and it starts with god existing, being one, and having no body. so a god with hair to comb isn't sweet to him. it's the exact mistake he wrote a whole book to get people out of.\nhe isn't sneering at the shepherd for being simple. he thinks the wrong picture puts you further from god, and that each thing you stop claiming gets you a little closer.","name":"maimonides","where":"the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 51 to 59, on what can and can't be said about god. his letters are the quick way to meet the man, a page or two each."},"masnavi":{"lines":"rumi's big book. six volumes, about twenty five thousand rhyming couplets in persian. he spoke it aloud over the last dozen or so years of his life, walking around konya, while his friend husam al-din wrote it down. sufis, the people inside islam who go after god directly, call it the quran in persian.\nit's stories inside stories. a king and a slave girl, a merchant and a parrot, dirty jokes, and then a passage that stops you cold. moses and the shepherd is one of them, in the second volume. it's a story in a poem, told to make a point, and everything god says in it is rumi's telling.\nthe page leaves out moses's argument, and it's a real one. hands and feet are fine for us, he says, but said of god they're filth. whatever has a body got born, and whatever got born isn't god. that's the right page, almost word for word. rumi gives the careful man his full say and then has god side with the shepherd anyway.\nit leaves out the ending too. moses runs into the desert after the shepherd and tells him he's allowed. no rules, no method, say what your heart needs to say. and the shepherd says he's already gone past that.\nthe old full english translation is nicholson's, a hundred years old and stiff, all thee and thou. there are newer ones that read much better.","name":"the masnavi","where":"the masnavi, book 2. the story starts at line 1720 in nicholson's numbering. god's answer to moses starts at line 1750."},"moses":{"lines":"the central figure of the hebrew bible, the jewish scriptures that christians call the old testament. the story is set maybe thirty three hundred years ago. as it's told, he led a nation of slaves out of egypt and came down a mountain with the law.\nhe belongs to muslims too. in the quran, islam's holy book, he's musa, a prophet and messenger of god, and he's named there more than anyone else, a hundred and thirty six times. his title in islam means the one god spoke to.\nthat's why rumi picked him. if anybody knew the right way to talk to god, it was the man god talked to directly. and in this story he's the one who gets it wrong.\none more thing. as the bible tells it, he was out tending his father in law's sheep when the bush caught fire and the voice came. the man he tells to shut up has his old job.","name":"moses","where":"exodus 3 and 4 in the bible for the bush and the argument. today's story about him is rumi's, in the masnavi, book 2."},"psalms":{"lines":"a book of the bible made of a hundred and fifty songs and prayers, many credited to king david. they cover everything. praise, thanks, rage, grief, and god why have you left me. they're still sung in synagogues and monasteries every day.\nso it's a strange place to find a line against talking. psalm 65 opens, word for word in the hebrew, something like: to you, silence, praise.\nmost english bibles smooth it out. the king james has praise waiteth for thee. the standard jewish translation has praise befits you. a few keep the silence in.\nmaimonides read it straight. silence is praise to you. a hundred and fifty songs to god, and he goes and finds the one line that says the best song is none.","name":"the psalms","where":"psalm 65, the first line after the heading. it's verse 2 in jewish bibles and verse 1 in most christian ones, because they count the heading differently."},"rumi":{"lines":"jalal ad-din rumi, 1207 to 1273. a muslim scholar and preacher in konya, a city in the middle of what's now turkey. a serious man with students, the kind of religious professor people came to for rulings on how to live.\nat thirty seven a wandering holy man named shams stopped him in the street, and the two of them were inseparable for a couple of years. his students hated it. then shams vanished for good. some say he was murdered, some say he just walked away. the scholar fell apart and came back a poet.\nhe spent the rest of his life turning that loss into poems, tens of thousands of lines. in america he shows up on wedding programs, in soft versions with the islam taken out. in the originals the god he's in love with is allah, and the lines are full of the quran, islam's holy book.\nthat's why he's on this page. he'd been the man with the rulings. he knew everything moses knows in this story, and he wrote it so the one who knows the rules is the one who gets corrected.","name":"rumi","where":"the masnavi for the stories. the essential rumi, coleman barks's collection, is the easy doorway, knowing it's loose. jawid mojaddedi's masnavi goes straight from the persian and keeps the rhymes."}},"right":{"lineage":"jewish","source":"maimonides, the guide for the perplexed, part one, chapters 58 and 59","sourceRef":"guideperplexed","text":"every word you say about god is wrong.\n[[maimonides]] was the sharpest jewish mind of the middle ages, and he'd have shut that shepherd up too.\nin [[guideperplexed|the guide for the perplexed]] he says you can't say one true thing about what god is. alive, wise, strong: human words, and they shrink him to your size. you can only say what he isn't.\nhe tells of a man leading prayers, great, mighty, awesome, piling it on, and [[hanina|a rabbi]] waiting for him to stop. finished? that's praising a king who owns millions in gold for having silver.\nthen a line from the [[psalms]]. to you, silence is praise.\nthe shepherd's god has lice. that ought to bother you."},"theme":"praying wrong","id":"2026-10-05"},{"caption":"the page one of them wrote is the curb the other one sat down on.","date":"2026-10-06","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261006bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--peach)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261006bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 266 L 150 266 M 752 266 L 800 266'/><path d='M 150 250 C 230 247 310 253 400 250 S 600 252 724 250 L 724 266 C 640 268 500 263 400 266 S 230 263 150 266 C 138 266 138 250 150 250 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='738' cy='252' r='14' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 738 252 m -6 0 a 6 6 0 1 1 6 6'/><path d='M 470 258 L 500 258 M 516 258 L 560 258 M 576 258 L 596 258 M 690 258 L 712 258'/><g opacity='.5'><path d='M 70 40 l -6 16 M 140 78 l -6 16 M 200 34 l -6 16 M 290 66 l -6 16 M 350 28 l -6 16 M 100 124 l -6 16 M 330 120 l -6 16 M 440 50 l -6 16 M 520 96 l -6 16 M 580 36 l -6 16 M 690 70 l -6 16 M 760 34 l -6 16 M 470 140 l -6 16 M 740 130 l -6 16'/><\/g><path d='M 168 250 L 168 218 L 214 218 L 214 250 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 182 218 C 182 207 200 207 200 218 M 168 230 L 214 230'/><circle cx='276' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 264 196 L 246 246 M 246 246 L 288 210 L 298 250 M 246 246 L 278 216 L 284 250 M 261 206 L 290 212 L 304 228'/><path d='M 308 243 L 330 243 L 336 245.5 L 344 245.5 L 344 248.5 L 336 248.5 L 330 250 L 308 250 Z' fill='var(--sage)'/><circle cx='640' cy='176' r='14'/><path d='M 640 190 L 640 232 M 640 232 C 620 232 610 248 625 250 M 640 232 C 660 232 670 248 655 250'/><path d='M 640 204 L 620 198 L 628 180 M 640 206 L 664 224 L 674 244 M 674 247 L 682 232'/><\/g><g transform='translate(88,248) scale(1.2)' fill='var(--page-ink)' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><path d='M0 5 Q 4 2 8 3 L 26 3 L 26 8 L 20 8 L 20 11 L 24 11 L 24 15 L 6 15 L 6 11 L 10 11 L 10 8 L 4 8 Z'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"christian","source":"brennan manning, all is grace · his last book, the memoir. then the ragamuffin gospel, chapter 1","sourceRef":"allisgrace","text":"he never got sober for good.\n[[manning|brennan manning]] was a priest who told crowds that god loves you as you are, not as you should be.\nhe was a drunk the whole time. rehab, relapse, rehab. he lied to the people who loved him. his marriage ended. by the end his brain was wrecked.\n[[allisgrace|his last book]] tells on him. the benders, the lies. he still calls all of it [[grace]].\nif that isn't true for the guy on the bender, it isn't true for anybody. then grace is a prize for people who were already fine."},"mode":"agree","question":"what have you sworn off more than once?","refs":{"allisgrace":{"lines":"brennan manning's memoir, from 2011, and his last book. he wrote it near the end with a co-writer, john blase, when he was old and sick. he died two years later.\nit admits what the crowds didn't know. he'd been drinking far more than anyone knew, all through the years he was on the road preaching grace, and lying to cover it. friends had been confronting him about the lying for years. it wasn't so much the big lies, he writes, as the little ones, the need to lie at all.\nit tells the marriage too. the last years before the divorce, he says, he was traveling way too much, isolated and drunk.\nwhat it doesn't do is take the message back. he calls his life a witness to vulgar grace, a grace that amazes and offends. to live by grace, he says, means owning the whole story, the light side and the dark.\nthe words all is grace are also how the diary of a country priest ends, a 1936 french novel about a sick young priest in a village that doesn't want him. they're the last thing he says as he dies.","name":"all is grace","where":"all is grace: a ragamuffin memoir, by brennan manning with john blase. philip yancey's foreword is the short version."},"grace":{"lines":"in christianity, grace is god's love that you don't earn and can't lose. it's a gift, not a paycheck. the word comes from the latin for free, as in on the house.\nthe whole fight of the protestant reformation, the split in the western church five hundred years ago, was partly over it. can you work your way to god, or does it only come as a gift. most christians say it's a gift and then live like it's a paycheck anyway, which is exactly what brennan manning spent his life yelling about.\noutside church the word survived in saying grace before a meal and in a grace period on a bill, the extra time you didn't earn. same idea.\nboth pages today are testing it. if it's a gift, it has to hold for the man who relapsed again last night. if it only holds once you're fixed, it was a paycheck all along.","name":"grace","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, the first chapter, is manning on it in plain english. paul's version is the letter to the romans."},"manning":{"lines":"born richard manning in new york in 1934. a marine, then a franciscan priest, ordained in 1963. the franciscans are an order of friars, men who promise to own nothing. he took the name brennan along the way. he was funny, charming, and a very good preacher.\nhe was also an alcoholic. he went into treatment in the seventies, got sober, relapsed, got sober, relapsed. in 1982 he left the franciscans and the priesthood to marry a woman named roslyn, which a catholic priest isn't allowed to do. so for the years he was famous he was an ex-priest. the marriage ended in divorce in 2000.\nin 1990 he wrote the ragamuffin gospel, the book that told a generation of church kids that god loved them as screwups, not after they got it together. then he spent twenty years on the road saying it to crowds. by his own account he'd stand up and preach it, check into a motel, drink himself into a stupor, and fly to the next talk when the bender was over.\nat the end he was nearly blind and could hardly speak or get around. the cause of death on record is wernicke-korsakoff syndrome, a brain disorder the drinking may have sped along. he died in 2013.\nthe line on this page was his whole message. he said it in talks for decades, and it's in the memoir too. he said it like a man who needed it to be true.","name":"brennan manning","where":"the ragamuffin gospel, the first chapter. then all is grace, which is the same man telling you what was going on while he wrote the first one."},"newtestament":{"lines":"the second, shorter part of the christian bible. twenty seven books written in greek in the first hundred years or so after jesus. four gospels about his life, the book of acts about the first christians, a pile of letters, and revelation, the wild vision at the end.\nthirteen of the twenty seven are letters with paul's name on them. that's where about half comes from. scholars are sure he wrote seven of those, romans among them. the rest are argued over, and some were probably written by his followers after he died.\nyou can read the whole thing in a few days. most of it is shorter than people expect.\nfor today: the book christians build everything on has this sitting in the middle of it. its busiest writer saying he can't make himself behave.","name":"the new testament","where":"any bible, the back part. the letters that are surely paul's include romans, the two to corinth, galatians and philippians."},"paul":{"lines":"a strict religious jew in the first century, from tarsus, near the south coast of what's now turkey. he went after the first followers of jesus, dragging them off to jail, until, as the book of acts tells it, a light knocked him flat on the road to damascus. he got up blind and switched sides.\nhe never met jesus in the flesh. he spent the next thirty years walking and sailing around the roman empire, starting little churches in people's houses, getting beaten, getting shipwrecked, and writing letters back to those churches when they screwed up, which was constantly. those letters are the oldest christian writing there is, older than the gospels.\nso when the page says he more or less built the church, and calls him the founder, that's what it means. christians would say jesus is the founder. paul is the one who carried it across the empire and wrote it down.\nhe had something wrong with him that he never names. a thorn in the flesh, he calls it. he says he begged god three times to take it away and got told, my grace is enough for you. he stopped asking.\nthat's the man talking in romans 7. tradition says rome cut his head off under the emperor nero.","name":"paul","where":"romans 7 and 8 for today. acts 17 for his speech in athens. then philippians, the whole thing. he wrote it from prison and it's short."},"romansletter":{"lines":"paul's longest letter, written around the year 57 to the christians in rome, a church he hadn't started and hadn't visited yet. it's his big statement of what he believed.\nchapter 7 is where he says he can't do the good he wants and keeps doing the thing he hates. he cries out, who will rescue me. then comes one line of thanks to god, and then he says the problem over again, flat: in my mind i serve god's law, and in my flesh i serve sin. chapter 8 opens right there, with no condemnation.\npeople have argued for a very long time about who the i is. one side says it's paul himself, a christian, right now. he switches from the past tense into the present for this stretch, and augustine, luther and calvin all read it that way. the other side says he's speaking as a man before christ, or without him, to show what the law can't fix, and that chapter 8 is the way out. serious scholars sit on both sides.\nthe page takes the first reading. it's fair to know there's a second.\nchapter 8 ends with nothing in all creation being able to separate us from the love of god. it's the letter that set off martin luther and the whole protestant reformation fifteen hundred years later.","name":"the letter to the romans","where":"romans 7:14 to 8:4 for today, then both chapters whole. the last two verses of chapter 8 are the ones people memorize."}},"right":{"lineage":"christian","source":"romans 7:15 to 8:1 · read 7 and 8 together, don't stop at the chapter break","sourceRef":"romansletter","text":"i do the thing i hate.\nabout half the books in the [[newtestament|new testament]] carry [[paul]]'s name. he more or less built the church. and in the middle of [[romansletter|his biggest letter]] he says this.\ni don't understand what i do. what i want to do, i don't. what i hate, i do.\npresent tense. then he just yells it. what a wretched man i am. who's going to get me out of this body.\nno plan follows. a verse later, no condemnation, and he says it before anything's fixed.\nthe man who built it had your problem. he never says it went away."},"theme":"the priest who kept drinking","id":"2026-10-06"},{"caption":"same body, same list. from one side it's lit up and from the other it's bones.","date":"2026-10-07","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261007bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--lavender)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--blue)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261007bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 90 253 180 259 280 256 S 460 254 560 256 S 720 258 800 256'/><circle cx='70' cy='60' r='18' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 26 256 l -6 -24 M 36 256 l 2 -34 M 46 256 l 9 -22 M 94 256 l -7 -26 M 104 256 l 1 -36 M 114 256 l 9 -20 M 224 256 l -7 -24 M 234 256 l 2 -32 M 244 256 l 9 -20 M 298 256 l -6 -22 M 308 256 l 2 -30 M 340 256 l 7 -18'/><circle cx='170' cy='112' r='14'/><path d='M 148 102 L 192 102 M 157 102 C 159 86 181 86 183 102'/><path d='M 170 126 L 170 190 M 170 190 L 156 254 M 170 190 L 186 254 M 170 142 L 216 120 M 170 144 L 150 168 L 160 184'/><path d='M 154 180 L 170 180 L 170 196 L 154 196 Z' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='400' cy='112' r='16' fill='var(--paper)'/><circle cx='407' cy='109' r='3.5'/><path d='M 403 122 l 0 5 M 408 121 l 0 5'/><path d='M 400 128 L 400 200'/><path d='M 400 144 L 368 118 M 400 200 L 382 254'/><path d='M 352 96 L 344 88 M 342 116 L 330 114 M 366 84 L 362 72 M 384 78 L 382 66 M 348 140 L 336 146'/><path d='M 400 156 C 409 153 416 156 419 162 M 400 168 C 409 165 416 168 419 174 M 400 180 C 408 177 414 180 416 185'/><path d='M 400 142 L 437 148 M 442 153 L 446 190 M 400 200 L 416 224 M 418 230 L 420 254'/><circle cx='440' cy='149' r='3'/><circle cx='417' cy='227' r='3'/><path d='M 548 256 C 540 236 544 222 556 214 L 584 214 C 596 222 600 236 592 256 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 556 214 C 560 206 580 206 584 214'/><circle cx='606' cy='250' r='1.6'/><circle cx='616' cy='252' r='1.6'/><circle cx='611' cy='244' r='1.6'/><circle cx='524' cy='252' r='1.6'/><circle cx='534' cy='247' r='1.6'/><circle cx='680' cy='180' r='14'/><path d='M 680 194 L 680 236 M 680 236 C 660 236 650 252 665 255 M 680 236 C 700 236 710 252 695 255 M 680 210 L 666 236 M 680 210 L 694 236'/><\/g><g transform='translate(58,229) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"other","source":"walt whitman, i sing the body electric, in leaves of grass · read the last section out loud","sourceRef":"bodyelectric","text":"your body is the holy thing.\n[[whitman|walt whitman]] wrote [[bodyelectric|a poem]] that reads out the body part by part like scripture. head, neck, ears. ribs, belly, hips. the armpit. the man-balls and the man-root. his words.\n[[leavesofgrass|the book]] most likely cost him a government job. later a boston prosecutor called it obscene and his publisher dropped it.\nhe didn't back off. if anything is sacred, he says, the human body is sacred. and these aren't only the parts of the body. they're the soul.\nyou were told the real you is somewhere else and this is the meat it rides in. he says no. the sweat is it."},"mode":"disagree","question":"which do you need to hear today, that it's holy or that it's meat?","refs":{"bodyelectric":{"lines":"a poem in nine sections from leaves of grass. it was one of the twelve poems in the first edition, in 1855, and like the rest it had no title. the next year he called it poem of the body and added the ending, the long list. the title it has now, and the first line that gives it, came in 1867.\nthe list is the last section. it starts o my body and then just names things, top to bottom. head, neck, hair, ears. upper-arm, armpit, elbow-socket. the lung-sponges, the stomach-sac, the bowels sweet and clean. food, drink, pulse, digestion, sweat, sleep, walking, swimming. womanhood, and all that is a woman.\nthe line about the body being sacred comes near the end of the section before it. the last two lines of the poem are the ones the page ends on. these are not the parts and poems of the body only, but of the soul. o i say now these are the soul.\nralph waldo emerson, the most famous american writer of the day and the man who'd written him the great fan letter, argued that he should cut the most sexual and bodily poems. whitman's account is that he heard him out and decided to disobey all and pursue his own way.\nso the list is the argument. he doesn't say the body is nice. he says there's nowhere else the soul is, and nothing on the list is too low to name.","name":"i sing the body electric","where":"leaves of grass, in the group of poems called children of adam. section 9 is the list. the sacred line is near the end of section 8."},"buddha":{"lines":"siddhattha gotama, born about twenty five hundred years ago near what's now the border of nepal and india. buddha isn't a name. it's a title, and it means the one who woke up.\nthe story, written down centuries later, says he was a rich man's son whose father tried to keep him from ever seeing anything sad. it didn't work. out in the town he saw an old man, then a sick man, then a corpse being carried off. at twenty nine he left home to find out why we suffer and whether it can stop.\nhe tried hating the body first. six years of starving himself, the way the holy men of his day did, and it got him nowhere but thin. he ate a bowl of rice, sat down under a tree, and woke up. then he taught for forty five years.\nso the man on this page isn't disgusted by bodies. he'd tried that and quit. the corpse is what sent him out the door in the first place, and here he's handing his monks the same sight on purpose.\nhe didn't ask anyone to believe him. he said try it and see.","name":"the buddha","where":"the satipatthana sutta for today. the dhammapada, a short book of his sayings in verse, is where most people start."},"leavesofgrass":{"lines":"walt whitman's book of poems, first printed at his own expense in 1855 with twelve poems, no author's name on the title page, and a picture of him in work clothes. it sounded like nothing before it. long loose lines, the body and sex and the whole country crammed in.\nhe spent the rest of his life adding to it and rewriting it. same title, a bigger book each time. the last edition, which he approved from his deathbed in 1892, had about four hundred poems.\nit got him in trouble twice. it's most likely what got him fired in washington in 1865, and in 1882 the district attorney in boston called it obscene literature and his publisher gave it up.\nit's the root of most american poetry since. and the part that upset people is the part on this page, a man naming his body out loud and calling it holy.","name":"leaves of grass","where":"leaves of grass, the last edition, from 1892, for the whole thing. the 1855 first edition is only twelve poems."},"satipatthana":{"lines":"a sutta is one of the buddha's recorded talks, memorized and chanted by monks for centuries before anyone wrote it down. this one is number 10 in the majjhima nikaya, the middle length talks, in the pali canon, the oldest complete collection of his teachings. the name means something like the foundations of mindfulness. he gives it to his monks in a town called kammasadhamma.\nit's instructions for paying attention to four things: the body, feelings, the mind, and the patterns his teaching points out. modern insight meditation is built on it, and the word mindfulness is a translation of the word in its title.\nthe body section starts gently, with breathing, then your posture, then whatever you happen to be doing. then it turns. thirty one things the body is made of, from head hair down to urine, sorted like grain out of a sack. then the body as earth, water, fire and wind, laid out like a butcher's cuts of a cow at a crossroads.\nthen the corpses, nine of them. as if you were to see one, it says, left out in the open. three days dead and bloated. picked at by crows and dogs. a skeleton with some flesh still on. bones scattered, bones gone white, bones gone to powder. after each one: this body too. such is its nature, such is its future.\nwhitman reads out his list and says holy. the buddha reads out nearly the same list and says look, it's just this. neither of them looks away.","name":"the satipatthana sutta","where":"majjhima nikaya 10. a longer version is digha nikaya 22. thanissaro bhikkhu's english translation is free to read; he calls it frames of reference."},"whitman":{"lines":"born on long island in 1819, raised in brooklyn, out of school at eleven. a printer's boy, a teacher, a newspaperman. at thirty six he paid to print a thin book of poems that sounded like nothing before it, loose and huge and in love with everybody. then he wrote rave reviews of it himself and got them printed without his name.\nduring the civil war he spent about three years in the army hospitals in washington, sitting with wounded and dying soldiers, bringing oranges and tobacco, writing letters home for the ones who couldn't. so when he talks about bodies he isn't being dreamy. he'd seen a great many of them torn up.\nin 1865 he had a clerk's job at the department of the interior. the new secretary, james harlan, came across his copy of leaves of grass at his desk, called the book obscene, and fired him the next day. harlan said afterward it was only about saving money, and the record is murky, but the book is most likely why. friends had him in another government job within a day.\nin 1882 the district attorney in boston told his publisher the book was obscene literature and sent a list of poems and lines to cut, this poem among them. whitman wrote back that the whole list was rejected and wouldn't be thought of under any circumstances. the publisher quit. he found another one, and that edition went through five printings of a thousand copies each.\nhe kept revising that one book until he died in 1892.","name":"walt whitman","where":"song of myself, sections 1, 6 and 52, to get his voice. then specimen days, his prose memoir, for the war hospitals."}},"right":{"lineage":"buddhist","source":"satipatthana sutta, majjhima nikaya 10 · the sections on the body, then the whole thing. it's the root of mindfulness practice","sourceRef":"satipatthana","text":"it's a sack of shit. literally.\nthe [[buddha]] gave his monks [[satipatthana|a meditation]] that goes through the body part by part too. hair, nails, teeth, skin. kidneys, bowels, shit, bile, snot, pus, blood, sweat, spit, piss.\nlook at it, he says, like a sack open at both ends, full of grain, and you're just sorting what's in it.\nfurther on. picture a corpse. three days dead, swollen, leaking. then one the crows and dogs have been at. then bones. each time you say, this body is the same kind of thing. it's headed there.\nit isn't hatred. it's so you'll quit being so damn impressed. with yours, and with hers."},"theme":"holy or a sack of meat","id":"2026-10-07"},{"caption":"the water that gets one of them back on his feet goes into the other one's cup, and he stops it short of full.","date":"2026-10-08","doodle":{"svg":"<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 800 320'><defs><linearGradient id='sf20261008bg' x1='0' y1='0' x2='1' y2='0'><stop offset='0' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.4' stop-color='var(--olive)'/><stop offset='0.6' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><stop offset='1' stop-color='var(--sage)'/><\/linearGradient><\/defs><rect x='0' y='0' width='800' height='320' fill='url(#sf20261008bg)'/><g fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='3' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round'><path d='M 0 256 C 90 253 180 259 280 256 S 460 254 560 256 S 720 258 800 256'/><path d='M 700 50 C 676 58 672 92 700 102 C 684 90 684 62 700 50 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 96 256 C 88 220 62 196 40 186 M 96 256 C 94 212 88 176 78 152 M 96 256 C 102 214 116 180 134 160 M 96 256 C 114 224 150 204 186 200'/><path d='M 52 188 l -6 -8 M 66 200 l -8 -6 M 84 170 l -8 -5 M 86 192 l 7 -6 M 120 182 l 8 -3 M 110 202 l 8 -4 M 150 208 l 4 -8 M 170 203 l 5 -8'/><circle cx='128' cy='252' r='3'/><circle cx='138' cy='253' r='3'/><circle cx='148' cy='252' r='3'/><ellipse cx='138' cy='244' rx='13' ry='5' fill='var(--marigold)'/><circle cx='176' cy='242' r='13'/><path d='M 172 244 q 3 3 6 0'/><path d='M 189 243 L 244 245 M 244 245 L 268 228 L 292 254 M 244 245 L 284 250 M 202 243 L 214 254 L 232 254'/><circle cx='330' cy='150' r='14'/><path d='M 330 164 L 330 214 M 330 214 L 318 256 M 330 214 L 342 256 M 330 180 L 364 172 M 330 182 L 312 206'/><path d='M 360 158 C 354 174 368 186 382 176 L 376 154 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><path d='M 382 168 C 404 162 432 178 451 204' stroke-dasharray='9 7'/><path d='M 441 206 L 445 226 L 459 226 L 463 206' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 444 217 L 460 217'/><circle cx='540' cy='184' r='14'/><path d='M 536 198 L 522 246 M 522 246 L 562 228 L 580 256 M 522 246 L 552 238 L 566 256'/><path d='M 532 212 L 494 224 L 463 218 M 532 210 L 508 196 L 504 174'/><path d='M 648 251 L 694 246 L 706 250 L 694 254 Z' fill='var(--paper)'/><path d='M 648 251 L 630 252'/><path d='M 722 256 L 724 246 L 752 246 L 754 256 Z' fill='var(--clay)'/><\/g><g transform='translate(122,133) scale(1.0)' fill='none' stroke='var(--page-ink)' stroke-width='1.6' stroke-linecap='round' stroke-linejoin='round' opacity='.85'><ellipse cx='12' cy='15' rx='8' ry='9'/><path d='M6 8 L4 2 L9 6 M18 8 L20 2 L15 6'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='3'/><circle cx='8.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><circle cx='15.5' cy='12' r='1' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M12 14 l -1.5 2.5 h 3 z' fill='var(--page-ink)'/><path d='M9 24 l -2 3 M15 24 l 2 3'/><\/g><\/svg>"},"left":{"lineage":"jewish","source":"1 kings 19:1 to 8 · read chapter 18 first, so you know what he'd just pulled off","sourceRef":"elijah","text":"take a nap.\n[[elijah]] had just won. fire from heaven, the whole country watching. then [[jezebel|the queen]] sent word he'd be dead by tomorrow, and he ran.\na day into the desert he sits under a bush and says, enough. kill me.\nthen he falls asleep.\ngod doesn't give him a speech. no toughen up, no count your blessings. [[angel|an angel]] touches him and says get up and eat. there's bread on hot stones and a jar of water. he eats and lies back down. the angel comes again. eat more. the road's too long for you.\na man asked to die, and heaven sent a snack and a second nap."},"mode":"agree","question":"is it really that bad, or are you just hungry and tired?","refs":{"angel":{"lines":"the hebrew word is malakh, and it means messenger. that's the whole word. the story gives it no wings and no glow. something touches him, says a few words, and there's food.\nthree verses earlier jezebel sends a messenger to elijah with the death threat. same hebrew word. english bibles call hers a messenger and god's an angel, but the text doesn't draw that line. one messenger tells him he'll be dead by tomorrow. the next one brings breakfast.\nlook at what this one says. get up and eat. the second time: get up and eat, or the road will be too much for you. nothing about god, no correction, nothing to believe. bread and water, twice.\nthat's why it's on this page. the first help heaven sends a man who wants to die is a meal and more sleep.","name":"the angel","where":"1 kings 19:5 to 7. jezebel's messenger is at 19:2."},"elijah":{"lines":"a prophet in the hebrew bible, from around the 800s bc. a prophet there isn't a fortune teller. it's someone who speaks for god, usually to people in charge who don't want to hear it. elijah is the loud one, fire and thunder.\nwhat he'd just pulled off: the queen had brought in a god called baal and was killing off the prophets of israel's god. elijah had the whole country called up to mount carmel for a contest. four hundred and fifty prophets of baal on one side and him, alone, on the other. whichever god answers with fire is god. his did. fire came down and burned up the offering, the wood and the stones.\nthe story doesn't stop at the win, and it isn't pretty. he had the four hundred and fifty seized and killed. then he ran ahead of the king's chariot through a downpour, all the way to the town of jezreel. the next thing he hears is the queen's message.\nso the man under the bush isn't soft. he's spent. he says he's no better than his fathers and asks to be done.\nafter the two naps and the two meals he walks forty days to a mountain, and there god does talk to him. not in the wind, not in the earthquake, not in the fire. in a small quiet sound after all of it. the food came first.","name":"elijah","where":"1 kings 18 for the fire on mount carmel. 1 kings 19 for the bush, the angel, and the quiet sound on the mountain."},"jezebel":{"lines":"the queen of israel in the 800s bc. she was a princess from the city of tyre, the daughter of its king, married to israel's king ahab.\nshe brought her own gods with her, baal and asherah. as the book of kings tells it, hundreds of their prophets ate at her table and she was killing off the prophets of israel's god. elijah's contest on the mountain was aimed at her, and it ended with him slaughtering her prophets.\nso her message answers in kind. may the gods do worse to me, she says, if by this time tomorrow i haven't made you like one of them.\nthat's all it takes. the man who'd just called down fire in front of the whole country hears one sentence from her and runs for his life. winning the big one didn't leave him anything for the day after.\nthe story was written by her enemies, and scholars say it shows. in it she ends up thrown from a window, and the dogs get her.","name":"jezebel","where":"1 kings 18 and 19. she's named at 18:4 and 18:19, and her message to elijah is 19:2."},"laotzu":{"lines":"the name given to the old chinese sage credited with the tao te ching. it just means old master. the dates people give him run from the sixth century bc to the fourth to never.\nthe story is that he kept the royal library for the zhou kings, got sick of the whole thing, and headed west. old paintings show him riding an ox. at the last gate out of the country the guard wouldn't let him leave until he wrote down what he knew. he wrote a short book and went on, and nobody knows where he ended up.\nthere may never have been a lao tzu. scholars are split on it, and the book is most likely a collection that grew over time. so when the page says lao tzu says, read it as the book says.\nthe legend fits today's chapter anyway. a man does his work at court for a long time, sees it's finished, and leaves without waiting around for the credit. do the work, then step back.","name":"lao tzu","where":"the tao te ching, chapters 1 through 4, then 8, 9, 11, 76 and 78. each chapter is a few lines."},"taoteching":{"lines":"the short chinese book credited to lao tzu. eighty one tiny chapters, about five thousand characters in all. the title means something like the book of the way and its power. it probably took shape over a couple of centuries before about 250 bc.\nit's about water, emptiness, not forcing, and how the soft outlasts the hard. it's also, oddly, advice to rulers, telling them the less they meddle the better things go.\nchapter 9 is five plain statements. a vessel is better left short of the brim. a point sharpened too far won't keep its edge. a hall full of gold and jade can't be kept safe. pride in wealth and rank brings its own disaster. when the work is done, withdraw. that last one, it says, is the way of heaven.\ntranslators don't agree on the wording, and you can watch it happen here. the old scholars' versions have a vessel you shouldn't fill. a loose modern one has a bowl that spills and a knife that goes blunt. the page takes its pictures from that one. the point doesn't move.\nit's one of the most translated books in the world. you can read the whole thing in an evening, and people spend their whole lives on it.","name":"the tao te ching","where":"tao te ching, chapter 9. d.c. lau's translation is the plain scholar's one, james legge's is the old one, stephen mitchell's is loose and pretty."}},"right":{"lineage":"tao","source":"tao te ching, chapter 9 · any translation. it's shorter than this page","sourceRef":"taoteching","text":"stop before it's full.\nthe [[taoteching|tao te ching]] gives it a few lines.\nkeep filling the cup and it spills. keep sharpening the blade and the edge won't last. fill the house with gold and you can't guard it. get proud of it and you've built your own fall.\ndo the work, then step back. that's the way of heaven.\nyou think the extra mile is where the virtue is. [[laotzu|lao tzu]] says the extra mile is where you ruin the knife.\nyou already ran the race. go to bed."},"theme":"eat something, go to sleep","id":"2026-10-08"}];
